{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghy1RX9n9pDekaEDdSmk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013DRJg85aQzZLNChZj5jonj","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","limit":420},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2651,"cache_read_input_tokens":22510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2651},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"ac8ea8d3-6fdd-461b-b8a6-c58200b996c6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:28:09.519Z","request_id":"req_011Cghy1RFnD41D8n7LPaWe4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013DRJg85aQzZLNChZj5jonj":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","limit":420}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DRJg85aQzZLNChZj5jonj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# CRICKET KERFUFFLE\n2\t\n3\t*An over-the-top arcade cricket game, where every ball causes a commotion and the tea is always stewed.*\n4\t\n5\tTitle picked as TASK.md asks: 50 playful riffs on \"cricket\" were brainstormed silently, then a real random number generator chose one (`python3 secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 47, entry #48: **Cricket Kerfuffle**). Don't re-roll it. A *kerfuffle* is a fuss or commotion, very British and very village cricket.\n6\t\n7\t**Core loop, in one sentence:** smash the ball or bowl it past the bat, run like a startled hen between the wickets, and win the match before the rain, the cow or Lord Thrumpton gets in the way.\n8\t\n9\tThis plan is for the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Its parts:\n10\t§1 deliverables and ground rules · §2 the game in brief · §3 architecture · §4 units and coordinates · §5 every system (each with how to test it) · §6 art · §7 animation · §8 content · §9 build order and milestones · §10 risks · §11 testing · §12 final checklist · §13 where each TASK.md requirement is met.\n11\t\n12\tNumbers in this plan are **starting values**. Each one marked *(T)* is a live dev-mode slider (§5.19), so tune by playing, not by guessing.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Deliverables, names and ground rules\n17\t\n18\t### 1.1 Names (use exactly)\n19\t- GameTitle token: `CricketKerfuffle`. SOURCE token: `Cricket`.\n20\t- Dev folder (all working files): `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825/`, inside the folder that holds TASK.md and this PLAN.md.\n21\t- The single-file game: `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in that same root folder, beside TASK.md.\n22\t- `exit-interview.json`, also in that root folder, beside the .html.\n23\t- If *your* TASK.md gives a different model or timestamp suffix, use your TASK.md's suffix with the same GameTitle and SOURCE tokens.\n24\t- On screen the title is **CRICKET KERFUFFLE**. In the AI Hub catalogue it is `Cricket Kerfuffle`.\n25\t\n26\t### 1.2 Sources to build on\n27\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, handoff package: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` (commit `4390a98` when this plan was written). Copy the whole `dist/handoff/` folder into `<dev>/engine/` and treat it as yours to change (§3.5 lists the planned engine changes).\n28\t- **AI Hub:** Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0**: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Download only the files you need with `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`: `README.md`, `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and `examples/parallel.html`. **Don't `git clone` ai-hub:** a shallow clone took over 5 GB of disk when this plan was written.\n29\t- **Read before coding.** Read these whole: `engine/API.md`, `engine/kits/sports.js` (the closest kit: ball as a Body, aftertouch, formations, a radar, commentary on Dialog's radio, a bot), `engine/docs/ASSETS.md`, `engine/modules/arcade.js` (tables, initials, attract helpers) and the hub `README.md`. In `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`, read these sections: *Standard features*, *Frame order*, *Scenes*, *Camera*, *Players*, *Motion and timing*, *TileMap*, *Humanoid*, *Audio*, *Text and UI*, *Lighting*, *Testing without a browser* and the final *Checklist*. Search `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n30\t- **Machine etiquette (from TASK.md).** Other agents share the machine.\n31\t  - Read only your own folder.\n32\t  - `/tmp` may be full, so clone into `<dev>/.cache/` or `~/.cache/<unique-name>/`.\n33\t  - When a server is needed, run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder to serve, parse the port it prints, and confirm the page is yours (fetch it and check for `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle`).\n34\t  - Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill or killall, and never kill whatever holds a port.\n35\t\n36\t### 1.3 Ground rules that shape everything\n37\t- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs: every feature in this plan ships complete. Where the plan offers a simpler fallback (§10), the fallback must be complete too.\n38\t- All audio is synthesized in code: no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish (§5.13).\n39\t- Controller first, for a TV seen from 10 feet away (§5.14 and §5.15). Keyboard and mouse must feel native. Touch-only devices get no touch controls (§5.15.6).\n40\t- The game runs from `file://` with no network requests. The AI Hub client only talks to the hub when the hub opened the page.\n41\t- The performance budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so FPS tuning is out of scope. Still avoid pathological designs, such as re-baking the floor every frame (§5.11).\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## 2. The game in brief\n46\t\n47\t| | |\n48\t|---|---|\n49\t| Genre | Arcade sports (cricket), over the top, with a comedy story campaign |\n50\t| Perspective | 2.5D in the engine's projected-3D views. A **broadcast camera** (TV-style, behind the bowler, looking down the pitch at the batter) for every delivery. A high **wide camera** that follows the ball round the ground. A side-on **review camera** for third-umpire replays and a **close-up camera** for celebrations. Story scenes use the three-quarter view. |\n51\t| Players | 1 to 4 on one machine: versus, co-op and 2v2, all on one shared screen. 1 to 4 on separate devices through AI Hub sessions (*Super Over Showdown*, parallel mode). |\n52\t| Tone | Village cricket's gentle eccentricity: tea, cake, drizzle, the vicar as umpire, a cow at deep midwicket. Delivered in the voice of the cricket commentary box (digressive, plummy, dry, given to long tangents) and of Wisden-style match reports. Then turned up to arcade bombast: flaming bats, triple-ball bowling machines, sixes through the pub window. |\n53\t| Session | Story campaign of about 40 minutes (5 chapters). A Quick Match takes 4 to 12 minutes. A Super Over Showdown takes 2 minutes. |\n54\t\n55\t**What the player can do:**\n56\t- Bat: aim with the stick or mouse; play a ground, lofted or defensive shot, or leave; charge down the pitch; use a signature special.\n57\t- Run: call runs, send the partner back, dive for the crease.\n58\t- Bowl: pick 1 of 3 deliveries or a special; place the pitching marker; time the release meter; add aftertouch swing or drift; choose the field; bowl over or round the wicket.\n59\t- Field: chase with the active fielder, dive, take catches on a timing ring, throw to either end for run-outs.\n60\t- Review an umpire's decision once per innings.\n61\t- Pick up arcade items, smash stadium targets, survive chaos events, go ON FIRE.\n62\t\n63\t**What the player can't do:** walk freely around the ground, edit teams or players, or play innings longer than 5 overs (no Test matches). In story mode the player always captains Kerfuffle CC. Each screen's help and the Nets mode make these limits clear.\n64\t\n65\t**Modes:** Story (*The Grand Kerfuffle*), Quick Match, Super Over Showdown (bat or bowl; high-score tables), Nets (practice, lessons and the \"Six Machine\" challenge), Versus (local 2–4), AI Hub session (parallel Super Over), plus Attract mode, Dev mode and About/Credits.\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 3. Architecture\n70\t\n71\t### 3.1 Stack\n72\t- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2**, the full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, every module built in). Use these parts:\n73\t  - `E.Game` scenes, boot steps, the standard pause menu, settings, controls, full screen and saves;\n74\t  - `E.TileMap` for the ground;\n75\t  - custom `E.View`s;\n76\t  - `E.Humanoid` rigs, `E.Attack` specs and `E.ACTIONS`;\n77\t  - `E.Body`, particles, lights and weather;\n78\t  - `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`, `E.font`, `E.ui`;\n79\t  - `game.script`, `game.tween`, `E.Path`;\n80\t  - the `arcade` module (`E.Arcade` tables and initials), the `story` module (`E.Story` for cutscene data) and the `songs` module (jingles);\n81\t  - the chip synth `game.audio`, plus our own Web Audio voice synth on the engine's audio context.\n82\t- **AI Hub session client 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified.\n83\t- **Node 18+** for the build and the headless tests. **Playwright 1.63.0** (pinned by `engine/package.json`) for the browser checks and the AI Hub harness.\n84\t- Nothing else. No libraries, no CDN, no fetch.\n85\t\n86\t### 3.2 Folder layout (inside `<dev>`)\n87\t```\n88\tengine/                 copy of dist/handoff (modified: see 3.5 and engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md)\n89\tvendor/aihub/           session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n90\tsrc/                    the game: plain script fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n91\t  00-head.js            'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; shared helpers (clamp, lerp, ...)\n92\t  01-const.js           units, geometry, enums (4)\n93\t  02-tuning.js          TUNING registry: defaults, difficulty multipliers, live get/set, persistence (5.19)\n94\t  03-rng.js             seeded streams: play, cosmetic, hub (3.4)\n95\t  04-bus.js             event bus: on(type, fn), emit(type, data); a 200-entry event log for dev mode\n96\t  05-store.js           profile: campaign progress, stars, unlocks, settings mirror, high-score ids\n97\t  06-assets-store.js    the few store entries used, cut by engine/tools/asset-set.mjs (6.3)\n98\t  07-assets-kerfuffle.js our new shapes and materials, as a standalone set file (6.5)\n99\t  10-data-teams.js      teams and players (8.2)\n100\t  11-data-grounds.js    ground recipes (8.3)\n101\t  12-data-deliveries.js delivery catalogue (8.4)\n102\t  13-data-shots.js      shot catalogue and the resolver table (8.5)\n103\t  14-data-specials.js   signature specials (8.6)\n104\t  15-data-arcade.js     items, stadium targets, chaos events (8.7)\n105\t  16-data-story.js      chapters, scenes, beats, goals (8.8)\n106\t  17-data-lines.js      commentary lines, match-report templates, UI copy (8.9)\n107\t  18-data-music.js      songs and stings (8.10)\n108\t  20-ball.js            ball physics and the trajectory predictor (5.1)\n109\t  21-bowling.js         delivery choice, marker, meter, release, aftertouch (5.2)\n110\t  22-batting.js         shot resolver, timing, contact, edges (5.3)\n111\t  23-running.js         running between wickets (5.4)\n112\t  24-fielding.js        fielders, interception, catches, throws, keeper (5.5)\n113\t  25-umpire.js          decisions, LBW, run-out, stumping, review (5.6)\n114\t  26-rules.js           match state, laws, scoring, scorecard (5.7)\n115\t  27-ai.js              CPU batting, bowling, captain, fielding, autopilot (5.8)\n116\t  28-hype.js            Hype meter, ON FIRE, specials runtime (5.9)\n117\t  29-chaos.js           items, targets, chaos events, weather, time of day (5.10)\n118\t  30-ground.js          TileMap builder from a ground recipe, floor texture, stands, crowd, panoramas (6.3)\n119\t  31-cast.js            rig factory: kits, gear, bat, faces, portraits (6.4)\n120\t  32-anim.js            pose library: stance, shots, bowling action, fielding, umpire signals (7)\n121\t  33-fx.js              trails, flames, stumps and bails, splinters, fireworks, confetti (6.6)\n122\t  34-camera.js          views and the camera director (5.11)\n123\t  35-replay.js          recorder, Owl-Eye replay, third-umpire screen (5.11)\n124\t  40-audio.js           SFX voices, crowd engine, music glue, stings (5.13)\n125\t  41-voice.js           gibberish speech synth (5.13.3)\n126\t  42-commentary.js      commentary director and captions (5.12)\n127\t  43-glyphs.js          button and key icons, device-following prompts (5.14.3)\n128\t  44-hud.js             match HUD (5.15.2)\n129\t  45-ui.js              10-foot widgets: big menus, cards, panels, sliders (5.15)\n130\t  50-title.js 51-attract.js 52-front.js (main menu, setup, versus lobby) 53-story.js (map, cutscenes)\n131\t  54-match.js (gameplay scene) 55-results.js 56-nets.js 57-showdown.js 58-about.js\n132\t  60-hub.js             AI Hub session flow (5.17)\n133\t  70-dev.js             dev panel (5.19)\n134\t  80-debug.js           game.debug: snapshot, force*, bot hooks (11.2)\n135\t  99-start.js           new E.Game(...), scenes, boot steps, game.start\n136\ttools/build.mjs         concatenate src -> dist/game.js; write dist/index.html (dev page) and the single-file game\n137\ttools/serve.mjs         start python3 http.server on port 0 at 127.0.0.1; return { port, pid, stop() }\n138\ttest/run.mjs            THE one test command (11.1)\n139\ttest/suites/*.mjs       the suites\n140\ttest/hub/               harness files + the built game, served for the harness run\n141\tdist/                   game.js, index.html (loads ../engine/my-3d2dge.js and game.js: quick iteration)\n142\tDESIGN-NOTES.md         short: how to add a team, a ground, a chapter, a delivery, a shot, a line\n143\t```\n144\t\n145\t### 3.3 How the code is organized\n146\t- **Simulation and presentation are separate.** The match simulation lives in `20–29`. It is pure and deterministic: fixed steps of 1/120 s, seeded RNG, no drawing and no audio. Presentation lives in `30–45`: it reads the simulation's state and listens on the event bus. Tests drive the simulation headless. The attract mode and the AI drive it through the same input interface a player uses.\n147\t- **Registries.** All content is data keyed by id: teams, players, grounds, deliveries, shots, specials, items, targets, events, chapters and lines. Systems look things up by id, and content never edits system code. Adding a ground means a new recipe in `11-data-grounds.js` plus any new shapes. `DESIGN-NOTES.md` says how, for each kind of content.\n148\t- **Event bus.** The rules emit events: `ball:released`, `ball:pitched`, `bat:contact {timing, shot}`, `ball:edge`, `ball:beaten`, `runs {n}`, `boundary {4|6}`, `wicket {how}`, `catch:dropped`, `appeal`, `decision {out, review}`, `extra {kind}`, `over:end`, `innings:end`, `match:end {result}`, `hype:full`, `fire:on|off`, `special {id}`, `item {id}`, `target:hit {id}`, `chaos {id}`, `rain:start|end`, `review:start|result`, `milestone {fifty|hattrick|maiden}`. Commentary, crowd, HUD, camera, Hype, achievements and the coverage tracker (§11) all subscribe.\n149\t- **TUNING.** One registry of the live gameplay variables. Systems read `T('ballSpeed')` at each use and never cache it, so dev sliders take effect at once.\n150\t- **The delivery director.** One state machine runs each ball (§5.7.3): `setup → runup → release → flight → inPlay → dead → between`. Each state has explicit exits and a timeout, so play can never stall.\n151\t- **Scenes** (`game.go(name, data)`): `title`, `attract`, `front` (main menu, mode select, setup, versus lobby), `storymap`, `cutscene`, `match` (pausable), `results`, `nets` (pausable), `showdown` (pausable), `about`, `hubcard` and `hubround` (pausable). Story, Quick Match, Showdown, Nets and the attract mode all run inside the `match` scene machinery, using a `MatchConfig` (§5.7.1).\n152\t\n153\t### 3.4 Determinism and randomness\n154\t- `RNG.play`: gameplay randomness (seam, AI errors, fielding). Seeded per match from `MatchConfig.seed`.\n155\t- `RNG.cosmetic`: crowd, particles and commentary-line choice, so gameplay never depends on them.\n156\t- In AI Hub sessions, the over's deliveries come from `AIHub.rng('over')` and each ball's fielding stream from `AIHub.rng('field' + n)`, so every copy faces identical balls whatever the player does (§5.17).\n157\t- Never call `Math.random` in simulation code. It is fine for engine-internal cosmetics.\n158\t\n159\t### 3.5 Planned engine changes\n160\tMake each change in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Log every one in `engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md`; About/Credits and the exit interview report them.\n161\t1. **Cricket bat:** a new Humanoid `weapon: 'bat'`. Add it next to the `o.weapon === 'sword'` branch in the HD draw, which uses `ops.push({ d, f })` with `J.handR`, `J.bladeDir` and `o.bladeLen`. It is a flat willow blade: length `o.batLen` (default 15 units: 5 handle, 10 blade), width 1.7 units, a darker edge line, a toe, and a coloured sticker band. Grip in `colors.grip`, blade in `colors.willow`. Give it a two-handed grip: the left hand IK'd to the handle, 2.5 units above the right hand (`E.ik3` from the left shoulder), applied in the draw pose. Add a smear for swings.\n162\t2. **Cricket gear:**\n163\t   - `pads: true`: white leg guards over the shins and knees, with buckles in `colors.trim`;\n164\t   - a `brim` hat style (umpire's and sun hats);\n165\t   - `hat: { style: 'helmet', grille: true }`: a batting helmet with a face grille;\n166\t   - `gloves: 'batting' | 'keeper'` for bigger hands;\n167\t   - `face: { moustache: 'handlebar' | 'walrus' | null, beard: 'full' | 'goatee' | null, glasses: true }`.\n168\t3. **Audio voice bus:** add `_voiceBus` beside `_sfxBus` and `_musBus`, a `voiceVolume` setting in the Sound group (label \"Voices\"), and `audio.bus(name)` returning the sfx, music or voice gain node, so the game's own Web Audio nodes follow the player's volumes.\n169\t4. **Button symbols:** in the standard settings table (`glyphs`, around line 2999), set the default to `'playstation'` and the options to `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox (A B X Y)']]`.\n170\t5. **Hub's Create + Options:** while `game.hubActive` is set and Pad8 (Create) is held, Pad9 (Options) must not open the pause menu.\n171\t6. **Floor bakes:** `TileMap._floor` keeps at most 6 baked floors (it clears the cache past that). Raise the limit to 10, so the four match cameras and the story views never re-bake (§5.11).\n172\t7. Any engine bug found along the way: fix it, and add one line about it to the log.\n173\t\n174\t### 3.6 Build and bundle (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n175\t1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { ... })();`, giving `dist/game.js`. Fail on a syntax error: run `new Function` on it in Node.\n176\t2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `game.js` as separate scripts (fast iteration).\n177\t3. Write the single file `../CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in exactly this order:\n178\t   - `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`;\n179\t   - **the session client** (`vendor/aihub/session.js`, byte-for-byte) as the first `<script>`;\n180\t   - viewport meta, `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle</title>`;\n181\t   - the hub tags (§5.17.1) and catalogue tags `hub:type`=`Game`, `hub:players`=`1-4`;\n182\t   - the page CSS (from `engine/game/index.html`), `</head><body>`;\n183\t   - the engine's boot splash markup (from `engine/game/index.html`, title CRICKET KERFUFFLE);\n184\t   - `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>`;\n185\t   - the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then `dist/game.js` inlined in a `<script>`.\n186\t   Escape any `</script>` inside the inlined code.\n187\t4. Print the size and the SHA-1 of the client block. The bundle test (§11) checks the block is identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js`.\n188\t\n189\t---\n190\t\n191\t## 4. Units, coordinates and constants (`01-const.js`)\n192\t\n193\t- **Engine convention:** 16 world units = 1 m (docs/ASSETS.md). x east (screen right), y south (screen down in yaw-0 views), z up.\n194\t- **The pitch** runs along x = 0. The **striker's stumps are at y = 0** (the far end, top of the broadcast screen). The **bowler's stumps are at y = 320** (20 m; the near end, bottom of the screen). The striker's popping crease is at y = 20 and the bowler's at y = 300. Return creases are at x = ±21. The pitch strip is 48 units wide (3 m).\n195\t- The camera always looks from behind the current bowler, so the bowling end is always at +y. At each change of ends, the batters swap logical places and the field is recomputed relative to the new striker. Visually, the \"far-end panorama\" (§6.3) switches to the other end's.\n196\t- **Stumps:** physics box x ∈ [−3, 3], z ∈ [0, 12], depth 1. Drawn slightly larger for readability: 6 units wide, 12.5 tall, with two bails.\n197\t- **Ball:** physics radius 0.6 units. Drawn as a 4×4 px sprite at minimum, with a 2 px shadow disc, a trail and a glow at night. Leather red `#b8202a` with a cream seam, white at night, pink at the Megadome.\n198\t- **Players:** Humanoid size 1.15–1.25 (about 28–30 units tall), so `E.HEIGHTS` defaults hold.\n199\t- **Batter's frame (handedness):** angles are measured from \"straight\" (toward the bowler, +y), positive toward the **off side**. A right-hander (RHB) faces −x in stance, so the off side is −x (screen left in the broadcast view). For a left-hander, everything mirrors (x → −x). Store `hand = +1` (RHB) or `−1` (LHB). The world direction for a batter-frame angle θ is `[−hand·sin θ, cos θ]`.\n200\t- **Boundary:** an ellipse per ground (rx × ry, about 560–760 units, i.e. 35–48 m) centred on the pitch midpoint (0, 160), scaled by `boundaryScale` *(T)*.\n201\t- **Physics constants:**\n202\t\n203\t  | Constant | Value |\n204\t  |---|---|\n205\t  | `gravity` *(T)* | 160 u/s² (real) |\n206\t  | `ballSpeed` *(T)* | scale 0.62 on nominal pace (a \"140 km/h\" ball travels at 386 u/s and reaches the bat in about 0.75 s) |\n207\t  | `pitchBounce` *(T)* | restitution 0.55 (times the ground's pitch factor) |\n208\t  | Outfield rolling decay | exp(−k·dt), k = 0.55 (ground factor; wet ×1.6) |\n209\t  | Grass bounce for hit balls | restitution 0.42 |\n210\t  | `runSpeed` *(T)* | 120 u/s |\n211\t  | `fieldSpeed` *(T)* | 105 u/s |\n212\t  | `throwSpeed` *(T)* | 420 u/s |\n213\t\n214\t  At these values a single takes about 2.9 s, and a fielder 30 m away gathering in 1.2 s makes a close run-out.\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 5. Systems and mechanics\n219\t\n220\t### 5.1 Ball physics and predictor (`20-ball.js`)\n221\t- **State:** `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, spin (rad/s about z, signed), swingA (lateral accel), age, phase: 'held'|'delivery'|'hit'|'rolling'|'dead', pitched, bounces, lastTouch: {kind, id}, carried (hit and not yet bounced), trail[] }`.\n222\t- **Step (1/120 s), in order:**\n223\t  1. Gravity: vz −= g·dt.\n224\t  2. While `phase === 'delivery'` and not yet pitched, add swing: a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's ground velocity, `swingA · min(1, age/0.35)` (late swing).\n225\t  3. Drift for spin.\n226\t  4. Drag: v ×= (1 − 0.02·dt).\n227\t  5. Integrate.\n228\t  6. Ground contact at z ≤ 0:\n229\t     - **On the pitch** (|x| ≤ 24 and y in −20..340), for a delivery: vz' = −vz · pitchBounce · ground.bounce · delivery.bounce. Horizontal speed × (0.86 − spinGrip). Turn the ground velocity by `turn° · ground.turn · T('spin')` plus the ball's stored seam deviation `ball.seamDev`. That deviation is drawn once **at release** from RNG.play (normal, σ = `seam` *(T)* · ground.seam · delivery.seam degrees) and kept on the ball. Emit `ball:pitched`.\n230\t     - **On grass** (any ball after the bat): bounce at 0.42; below 25 u/s vertically it becomes `rolling`, with decay k.\n231\t  7. Boundary check (§5.7.4).\n232\t  8. Target and item checks (§5.10).\n233\t  9. Stumps check (§5.6).\n234\t- **Solving a delivery** (`solveDelivery(release, target, kmh, deliveryType)`): given the release point (x = ±8 for over or round the wicket, y = 300, z = 34), the pitch target (tx, ty) and the speed, find the initial velocity that lands at the target *without* swing, using `E.arc`-style closed-form projectile maths with the delivery's dip. Swing and aftertouch then move the real pitching point. That is the point of swing, and what the batter must read.\n235\t- **Predictor** (`predict(ballState, seconds, opts)`): runs the same step function on a copy and returns sampled points `{t, x, y, z, phase}` every 1/120 s. It is used for:\n236\t  - the batting timing (when the ball reaches the contact plane);\n237\t  - the AI's reads;\n238\t  - fielder interception;\n239\t  - the landing point of a catch;\n240\t  - LBW (the path continued past the pad);\n241\t  - the Owl-Eye tube.\n242\t\n243\t  The step function draws no random numbers (the seam deviation is already on the ball), so the prediction equals reality exactly. The AI adds its own perception error on top.\n244\t- **Tests (unit-ball):**\n245\t  - 50 random solved deliveries without swing pitch within 1 unit of the target;\n246\t  - an outswinger to a right-hander lands on the off side of the target (−x) and an inswinger on the leg side;\n247\t  - a leg break pitched on middle turns toward −x for a right-hander;\n248\t  - the predictor matches the stepped simulation within 0.01 units over 3 s;\n249\t  - rolling balls stop;\n250\t  - a ball dropped from z = 50 bounces lower each time.\n251\t\n252\t### 5.2 Bowling (`21-bowling.js`)\n253\t**The flow for a human bowler:**\n254\t1. **Between balls**:\n255\t   - R1 (Space) cycles the field preset (Attacking, Balanced, Defensive, or Spin attack for spinners);\n256\t   - L1 (Shift) switches over or round the wicket;\n257\t   - the stick, WASD or the mouse moves the **pitch marker**: a ring on the pitch, clamped to y ∈ [20, 230] and |x| ≤ 30, with labelled length bands *yorker / full / good / short / bouncer* drawn faintly on the pitch the first few overs;\n258\t   - the delivery panel at the bottom shows the bowler's 3 deliveries on ✕ □ ○ and the special on △ (greyed out until Hype is full).\n259\t2. Pressing a delivery button starts the **run-up**: pace bowlers run 2.0 s, medium 1.5 s, spinners 0.9 s. The marker can still move during the run-up, at half speed.\n260\t3. **The release meter**: during the last 0.7 s, a needle sweeps a bar left → right → left at `meterSpeed` *(T)* (1.4 Hz). The centre zone (width `meterSweet` *(T)* 0.18 of the bar, scaled by bowler skill) is green. Press any delivery button or click (✕/J/LMB) to release:\n261\t   - **green**: accuracy scatter ±2 units, pace and spin 100–105%;\n262\t   - **amber**: scatter ±10, 90%;\n263\t   - **red edge**: scatter ±18, 80%;\n264\t   - **the far-right red tip** (the last 6%) is an **overstep**: a no-ball and a FREE HIT;\n265\t   - **no press**: auto-release at the end with an amber result.\n266\t4. **Aftertouch**: for 0.25 s after release, the stick's horizontal push adds swing (seamers) or drift (spinners), up to the delivery's allowance ±0.6. The mouse's horizontal movement does the same.\n267\t5. **Specials** (△ when Hype is full) skip the meter (always green) and add their effect (§8.6).\n268\t\n269\t**CPU bowlers** use the same pipeline: the AI chooses a delivery and a target (§5.8), its meter press lands at a skill-based offset, and the result goes through the same code.\n270\t\n271\t**Delivery types** are data (§8.4): pace, swing, spin and medium families, each with nominal km/h, length band limits (a bouncer pitches ≥ 140 units from the batter's crease), swing, seam, turn, bounce and dip multipliers, FX and AI weights. The HUD shows the speed after release (\"143 KM/H\") and the delivery name in small type.\n272\t\n273\t**Wides and no-balls:**\n274\t- A **wide** is a ball passing the striker's popping crease more than 26 units to the off side or more than 18 to the leg side of the middle-stump line (x = 0, measured in the striker's frame), without touching the bat or the batter. A ball passing above z = 40 (a head-high bouncer) is also a wide.\n275\t- A **no-ball** comes from an overstep, or a full toss above z = 26 at the crease (\"a beamer\": over the top, the commentators gasp).\n276\t\n277\t**Tests:**\n278\t- meter green → pitches within 3 units of the marker;\n279\t- red tip → `extra:noBall` and the next ball is `freeHit`;\n280\t- no input → amber auto-release;\n281\t- aftertouch right → pitch point moves +x;\n282\t- run-up durations per family;\n283\t- a field-preset cycle changes the fielder targets;\n284\t- over/round moves the release x.\n285\t\n286\t### 5.3 Batting (`22-batting.js`)\n287\t- **Stance:** the striker takes guard with the body 8 units to the leg side of the stumps line, side-on, the bat grounded and the head turned to the bowler (§7). The non-striker backs up at the bowler's end.\n288\t- **Inputs:**\n289\t  - **aim** (the stick, WASD, or the mouse's ground point relative to the batter via `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse wins while it moved in the last second, else the stick). Aim is **in screen space**: push toward where the ball should go as you see it, turned into a ground direction with `game.view.screenDirToGround`. Down is straight back past the bowler, left and right go across the field, up goes behind the batter (fine). An aim arrow on the grass at the batter's feet shows the direction live;\n290\t  - **shot** ✕/J/LMB (ground), **loft** □/K/RMB (in the air), **block** ○/L/Q (defend);\n291\t  - **special** △/I/F;\n292\t  - **charge** R2/E: before the ball pitches, step 24 units down the pitch. This turns a good length into a half-volley, but a miss off a spinner means a stumping chance.\n293\t  - No button means a **leave**: the bat goes up. Against a bouncer above z = 28 the batter ducks or sways by himself when leaving or blocking.\n294\t- **Timing model:** each shot in the catalogue has `contactAt` (seconds from press to the bat's contact moment, e.g. a drive 0.13 s, a cut 0.11, a block 0.08). The predictor gives `tArrive`, when the ball reaches the shot's contact plane (front-foot shots y ≈ 36, back-foot y ≈ 14; a charge moves it). With Δ = (tPress + contactAt) − tArrive:\n295\t\n296\t  | Grade | Window | Result |\n297\t  |---|---|---|\n298\t  | PERFECT | \\|Δ\\| ≤ `perfectMs` *(T)* 40 ms | full power, true direction |\n299\t  | GOOD | ≤ `goodMs` *(T)* 85 ms | 85% power, ±6° error |\n300\t  | EARLY / LATE | ≤ `okMs` *(T)* 150 ms | 60% power; direction pulled (early: toward leg) or sliced (late: toward off / behind), launch +8° (skier risk) |\n301\t  | MISS | beyond 150 ms | beaten: the ball goes on to pads, stumps or keeper |\n302\t\n303\t  Presses made after the ball passed the contact plane are ignored. Show a big timing word for 0.6 s (PERFECT in gold, GOOD, EARLY, LATE), on by default.\n304\t- **Shot resolver:** maps (button, stick sector in the batter's frame, ball length at pitch, ball line and height at contact) to one shot from the catalogue (§8.5). For example: ✕ + off sector + full ball = cover drive; ✕ + leg + short = pull; □ + leg + short = hook; □ + behind = scoop; □ + straight vs a yorker = helicopter. A neutral stick uses the shot's natural direction for that ball.\n305\t- **Reach check:** each shot has a contact zone (line range and height range relative to the batter). Outside the zone by up to `edge` *(T)* × 4 units gives an **edge**:\n306\t  - a thin edge goes to the keeper or slips: speed 35–55% of the incoming, deflected 150–175°, slight rise;\n307\t  - a thick edge goes to third man or gully;\n308\t  - an inside edge goes onto the pad or stumps (\"dragged on\").\n309\t\n310\t  Further outside is a **miss**.\n311\t- **The outgoing ball:**\n312\t  - direction: the aim direction, else the shot's natural direction, plus the timing error, plus RNG.play noise of σ 2°;\n313\t  - speed: `shot.speed × batter.power × timingFactor × (1 + 0.25·incoming/386) × T('batPower' or 'loftPower')`, × 1.2 when ON FIRE;\n314\t  - launch angle: from the shot (ground 2–10°, lofted 28–48°), plus timing (early lofted shots go higher).\n315\t\n316\t  Ground shots bounce and roll. Lofted shots carry.\n317\t- **Visual alignment:** the swing's animated contact is timed to `contactAt` through `E.Attack` phases (the wind phase ends at contact). During the last 30 ms, the ball's *drawn* position is pulled toward the bat's sweet spot, cosmetically only. Contact spawns splinters or sparks at the bat and plays the \"tock\".\n318\t- **Tests (unit-shots, sim):**\n319\t  - every resolver row gives its shot;\n320\t  - Δ = 0 gives PERFECT and Δ = 60 ms gives GOOD;\n321\t  - a press 200 ms early misses;\n322\t  - a ball 6 units outside a drive's zone edges behind;\n323\t  - stick straight plus PERFECT ✕ on a half-volley gives a direction within ±10° of straight;\n324\t  - a charge then a miss vs spin with the keeper up gives a stumping;\n325\t  - leaving a ball on the stumps gets the batter bowled;\n326\t  - leaving a ball 20 units outside off is safe;\n327\t  - ducking a bouncer leaves the batter not out with no wide called unless z > 40.\n328\t\n329\t### 5.4 Running between wickets (`23-running.js`)\n330\t- **Calls:** after contact (or byes and leg byes when the ball beats the bat and gets past the keeper), the striker's call is shown in a speech bubble: **YES / NO / WAIT**, with a glyph prompt \"R1 RUN\" or \"L1 STAY\". The human presses **R1/Space** to run, **again** at the crease to turn for another run, and **L1/Shift** to send both back. **R1 within 50 units of the crease** while a throw is coming in makes the batter **dive**: the bat slides the last 20 units, so the bat is grounded 0.15 s sooner, and he gets up after 0.6 s.\n331\t- **The partner:** the non-striker is the AI (1P), or the second human in co-op (§5.16.5). The AI partner follows the human's YES and NO. In co-op both must call YES within 0.4 s, or there's a **mix-up**: both may run to the same end, and whoever left his crease is the one run out. The two gibberish shouts overlap.\n332\t- **Movement:** batters accelerate at 600 u/s² to `runSpeed` *(T)*, turn at the crease in 0.25 s, carry the bat, and slow 15% on sand or wet ground. Runs count as each batter grounds the bat beyond the far popping crease; a short run is not modelled.\n333\t- **AI calling** (also used for the YES / NO / WAIT bubble hint): compare the batters' time to complete the next run with the fielding side's earliest time to break the stumps at the far end (§5.5 interception plus throw time). Margin > 0.35 s gives YES. Margin < −0.15 s gives NO. In between it's WAIT, and the CPU decides by aggression.\n334\t- **Tests:** a push to cover with R1 once gives 1 run and the strike rotates; R1 twice on a slow outfield shot gives 2; L1 mid-run sends both back to their crease; a co-op mix-up gives exactly one run-out; a dive beats a throw arriving 0.1 s after the undived time.\n335\t\n336\t### 5.5 Fielding (`24-fielding.js`)\n337\t- **Positions:** field presets are lists of `{ pos: 'slip1', θ, d }` in the batter's frame (§8.4.2), so a left-hander gets the field mirrored. Between balls, fielders walk to their spots (and teleport if more than 3 s away). As the bowler runs in they **walk in** a few steps, crouched (the engine pose `crouch`, with forward motion).\n338\t- **Interception:** on contact, the predictor samples the ball every 0.05 s. For each fielder, find the earliest t where dist(fielder, ball(t)) ≤ fieldSpeed·(t − reaction 0.18 s) + reach (catch reach 9 for aerial balls at z ∈ [4, 40]; stop reach 6 on the ground; dive +18).\n339\t  - The best fielder **chases**, the second-best **backs up**, the keeper and bowler run to their stumps, and the rest hold.\n340\t  - If no interception exists before the boundary, the nearest deep fielder chases anyway (it reads well) and a FOUR or SIX follows.\n341\t- **Catches** (the ball carried from the bat):\n342\t  - **CPU side:** success p = clamp(fielder.catch × difficulty − 0.25·(ball speed/400) − 0.3·(dive ? 1 : 0), 0.05, 0.97), drawn from RNG.play.\n343\t  - **Human side** (Manual or Assisted fielding, §5.15.5): the **catch ring** QTE. An outer ring shrinks onto an inner ring at the catch point over `catchRing` *(T)* 0.9 s. ✕/J/LMB within `catchWindow` *(T)* ±110 ms of the overlap catches, and a PERFECT press with a dive becomes a \"SCREAMER!\" with slow-mo. Early or late is a juggle (50% then a 2nd ring of 0.4 s). No press or a miss is DROPPED. In Assisted mode, easy chances (p > 0.85) are caught automatically.\n344\t- **Ground fielding:** the fielder gathers when the ball is within reach: 0.25 s for a stop, a dive stop on ✕ if the ball passes within 18 units. A misfield chance (1 − fielder.ground) × 0.1 lets the ball through.\n345\t- **Throws:**\n346\t  - **Human:** after gathering, **✕/J/LMB throws to the striker's end** (the keeper's end), **□/K/RMB to the bowler's end**, **○/L/Q lobs back to the keeper** (no shy). With the mouse, clicking either set of stumps in the world (or its icon) throws there. A press within 0.3 s of gathering is a \"rocket\": throw speed +20%.\n347\t  - **CPU:** throws to the end with the better run-out margin.\n348\t  - The throw follows an `E.arc` toward the stumps with error N(0, `throwError` *(T)* × distance/480). A **direct hit** is when the path crosses the stumps box. Otherwise the keeper or bowler, if within 10 units of the stumps, collects and breaks them in 0.2 s. A missed shy with nobody backing up gives **overthrows**: the ball runs on, and the batters may run more.\n349\t- **Keeper:** stands 160 units back to pace and 24 units (standing up) to spin and medium. Catches edges (CPU p from keeper skill; human edges use the catch ring too), collects deliveries, stumps after a charge-and-miss (0.25 s), and receives throws.\n350\t- **Tests:**\n351\t  - an interception is found for a ball hit straight at mid-off;\n352\t  - nobody intercepts a perfectly placed cover drive into a gap at 450 u/s on a fast outfield → FOUR;\n353\t  - a CPU catch with p = 0.97 under a seeded RNG gives \"caught\";\n354\t  - a human ring press at the overlap catches, and no press drops;\n355\t  - a ✕ throw with the striker 30 units short and a direct hit is OUT;\n356\t  - a missed shy with no backup gives ≥ 1 overthrow;\n357\t  - no fielder is ever more than 15 s in one state (stuck check, run in bot-match).\n358\t\n359\t### 5.6 Umpiring and dismissals (`25-umpire.js`)\n360\t- **Bowled:** the ball enters the stumps box with bails on (the stumps check in the ball step); the bails fly as physics sprites. **Hit wicket:** a back-foot shot with an EARLY/LATE grade has a 1.5% chance; the bat clips the stumps, comically. **Caught:** §5.5. **Run out:** stumps broken while the batter heading to that end hasn't grounded his bat. **Stumped:** the keeper breaks the stumps while the striker is out of his ground after a miss, and not attempting a run.\n361\t- **LBW:** if the ball meets the front pad box before the bat (box around the leading leg: 6×4×16), evaluate the truth:\n362\t  1. pitched outside leg stump → not out;\n363\t  2. impact outside off stump *and* a shot was offered → not out;\n364\t  3. the predictor continues from the impact with the pad removed: does it hit the stumps box (inflated by the ball radius)? → out.\n365\t\n366\t  If the predicted path is within 1 unit of the stumps' edge, the result is **UMPIRE'S CALL**: the on-field decision stands on review.\n367\t  - **The on-field decision:** the umpire is \"right\" with probability `umpire.accuracy` (Rev. Hollis 0.86, others 0.92), from RNG.play. Wrong decisions are what reviews are for.\n368\t- **Appeals:** the fielding side appeals (\"HOWZAT!\" in gibberish, arms up) for every LBW candidate, caught-behind edge and close run-out. Umpire signals follow (§7).\n369\t- **The Kerfuffle Review System (KRS):** each side has 1 review per innings. After a decision, the batting side (on OUT) or the fielding side (on NOT OUT) has 4 s to press **△/I/F \"REVIEW\"** (the CPU reviews when its truth confidence > 0.7). The review plays the **Owl-Eye** replay (§5.11.4): the ball path tube, the pitching and impact markers, a three-light verdict (PITCHING, IMPACT, WICKETS), each green, red or amber for umpire's call. Then OUT or NOT OUT is stamped like a slot machine. A successful review is kept; a failed one is lost.\n370\t- **Third umpire** (automatic, no review used): run-outs and stumpings with a margin under 0.15 s, and catches where the ball touched the ground within 0.05 s of the catch, are sent upstairs. The side-on review camera replays in slow-mo and a big screen reveals OUT or NOT OUT.\n371\t- **Tests (unit-umpire):** the three LBW truth rules; umpire's call; the accuracy draw is seeded; a review overturns a wrong decision and is kept; a failed review is lost; a run-out margin of 0.1 s triggers the third umpire; a stumping requires the striker out of his ground.\n372\t\n373\t### 5.7 Match rules and scoring (`26-rules.js`)\n374\t\n375\t#### 5.7.1 MatchConfig and state\n376\t```js\n377\tMatchConfig = { mode: 'story'|'quick'|'showdown'|'nets'|'attract'|'hub'|'versus',\n378\t  teams: ['ker','mud'], ground: 'green', overs: 2, wickets: 5, ballsPerOver: 6, maxOversPerBowler: 'auto',\n379\t  difficulty: 'village'|'county'|'test'|'kerfuffle', kerfuffleRules: true, timeOfDay: 'day'|'dusk'|'night',\n380\t  weather: 'clear'|'overcast'|'rain'|'fog', toss: true, scenario: null | { innings, target, battingFirst, preset },\n381\t  humans: { bat: [inputIndex...], bowl: [...] }, seed, superOver: true, reviewsPerInnings: 1, chapter: null }\n382\t```\n383\t- `maxOversPerBowler: 'auto'` means ceil(overs / 5): one over each for 1–5-over innings, so several bowler types appear.\n384\t- The match state holds the innings array: runs, wickets, legal balls, extras `{wd, nb, b, lb}`, batter cards (runs, balls, 4s, 6s, how out), bowler cards (balls, runs, wickets, maidens), ball records, partnership, target, revised target, free-hit flag, reviews left, Hype, ON FIRE, items held, and the result.\n385\t- `rules.applyBall(state, outcome) → { state, events[] }` is a **pure function**. Every ball record is kept for the results screen, the highlights and the report.\n386\t\n387\t#### 5.7.2 The laws (arcade cut)\n388\t- An over is 6 legal balls. Wides and no-balls add 1 run and are re-bowled.\n389\t- **FREE HIT** after a no-ball: the only dismissal is run out. The umpire circles his finger and the HUD flashes FREE HIT.\n390\t- The strike changes on odd runs and at the end of each over.\n391\t- An innings ends at the overs limit, the wickets limit, or (2nd innings) when the target is passed.\n392\t- **Result:** won by N runs (side batting first) or by N wickets (chasing side), with \"B balls to spare\" text. A **tie** goes to a **Super Over**: each side bats 1 over with 2 wickets, the side batting second in the match bats first, and if it's tied again the side with more boundaries in the Super Over wins. This is the real rule simplified, and it is shown as a tooltip.\n393\t- Byes and leg byes come from running after a miss (leg bye if it hit the pad).\n394\t- Milestones: fifty (rare in short games, but supported), hat-trick (three wickets in three of a bowler's balls) and maiden.\n395\t\n396\t#### 5.7.3 The delivery director (per ball; every state has a timeout)\n397\t\n398\t| State | Contents | Exit |\n399\t|---|---|---|\n400\t| `setup` | fielders to positions, batter guard, bowler at the mark; human chooses delivery/marker or waits; HUD prompts; CPU bowler \"thinks\" 0.6 s | the bowler starts the run-up (human press, or CPU after 0.6–1.2 s); 20 s idle in Quick Match shows a nudge prompt |\n401\t| `runup` | bowler runs; marker; meter; charge allowed | release |\n402\t| `release`/`flight` | ball spawned; aftertouch 0.25 s; batter swing window | contact, miss, leave, or the ball reaching y < −30 |\n403\t| `inPlay` | fielders chase; batters run; throws; catches; boundary | dead-ball condition |\n404\t| `dead` | umpire signal; appeals; decision; review window; replay (if enabled) | done, or 12 s cap |\n405\t| `between` | score update, events, commentary, over end, bowler select (human: big portrait picker), innings end | next `setup` |\n406\t\n407\t**Dead-ball conditions:** the ball crosses the boundary; a catch is completed; bowled; a run-out or stumping is completed; the ball is settled (in the keeper's or bowler's hands, or a fielder within 32 units of the stumps holding it, with both batters grounded, for 0.8 s); or a hard cap of 15 s in `inPlay` (a fielder fetches it and the umpire calls dead).\n408\t\n409\t**Pacing:** between balls takes ≤ 2.5 s excluding replays. Replays and celebrations are skippable with ✕/J/LMB.\n410\t\n411\t#### 5.7.4 Boundaries\n412\tWhen the ball crosses the boundary ellipse: if it touched the ground since leaving the bat (or was rolling), it's FOUR; if it carried over on the full, SIX. A ball hit over the boundary keeps flying for the show: into the stands (crowd scatter, a spectator catch \"Crowd catch!\", a cheer), into a stadium target (§5.10), or into the sea or pub.\n413\t\n414\t#### 5.7.5 The Kerfuffle Method (rain rule; Dustbowl Downs, and a dev-mode trigger)\n415\tWhen rain cuts the overs of the 2nd innings: resources R(o, w) = (o / O) · (1 − 0.07·w), where o = overs left, O = overs at the start of the innings and w = wickets lost. Revised target = floor(firstInningsRuns × R_after / R_before) + 1, never less than the current score + 1. It is shown on a chalkboard card by Mrs Crumb with absurd working (\"carry the scone\") and a big new target. Test it with fixed numbers.\n416\t\n417\t#### 5.7.6 Tests (unit-rules)\n418\tLegal ball counting with wides and no-balls; free hit; strike rotation on 1, 2 and 3 runs and at over end; each innings-end condition; result texts; the Super Over trigger and its tie-break; the Kerfuffle Method numbers; max overs per bowler; the scorecard totals equal the sums of the ball records.\n419\t\n420\t### 5.8 AI (`27-ai.js`)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"0a75fc54-ed54-446d-8c7c-81e0fc8145c8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:28:09.671Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","content":"# CRICKET KERFUFFLE\n\n*An over-the-top arcade cricket game, where every ball causes a commotion and the tea is always stewed.*\n\nTitle picked as TASK.md asks: 50 playful riffs on \"cricket\" were brainstormed silently, then a real random number generator chose one (`python3 secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 47, entry #48: **Cricket Kerfuffle**). Don't re-roll it. A *kerfuffle* is a fuss or commotion, very British and very village cricket.\n\n**Core loop, in one sentence:** smash the ball or bowl it past the bat, run like a startled hen between the wickets, and win the match before the rain, the cow or Lord Thrumpton gets in the way.\n\nThis plan is for the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Its parts:\n§1 deliverables and ground rules · §2 the game in brief · §3 architecture · §4 units and coordinates · §5 every system (each with how to test it) · §6 art · §7 animation · §8 content · §9 build order and milestones · §10 risks · §11 testing · §12 final checklist · §13 where each TASK.md requirement is met.\n\nNumbers in this plan are **starting values**. Each one marked *(T)* is a live dev-mode slider (§5.19), so tune by playing, not by guessing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Deliverables, names and ground rules\n\n### 1.1 Names (use exactly)\n- GameTitle token: `CricketKerfuffle`. SOURCE token: `Cricket`.\n- Dev folder (all working files): `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825/`, inside the folder that holds TASK.md and this PLAN.md.\n- The single-file game: `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in that same root folder, beside TASK.md.\n- `exit-interview.json`, also in that root folder, beside the .html.\n- If *your* TASK.md gives a different model or timestamp suffix, use your TASK.md's suffix with the same GameTitle and SOURCE tokens.\n- On screen the title is **CRICKET KERFUFFLE**. In the AI Hub catalogue it is `Cricket Kerfuffle`.\n\n### 1.2 Sources to build on\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, handoff package: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` (commit `4390a98` when this plan was written). Copy the whole `dist/handoff/` folder into `<dev>/engine/` and treat it as yours to change (§3.5 lists the planned engine changes).\n- **AI Hub:** Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0**: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Download only the files you need with `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`: `README.md`, `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and `examples/parallel.html`. **Don't `git clone` ai-hub:** a shallow clone took over 5 GB of disk when this plan was written.\n- **Read before coding.** Read these whole: `engine/API.md`, `engine/kits/sports.js` (the closest kit: ball as a Body, aftertouch, formations, a radar, commentary on Dialog's radio, a bot), `engine/docs/ASSETS.md`, `engine/modules/arcade.js` (tables, initials, attract helpers) and the hub `README.md`. In `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`, read these sections: *Standard features*, *Frame order*, *Scenes*, *Camera*, *Players*, *Motion and timing*, *TileMap*, *Humanoid*, *Audio*, *Text and UI*, *Lighting*, *Testing without a browser* and the final *Checklist*. Search `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n- **Machine etiquette (from TASK.md).** Other agents share the machine.\n  - Read only your own folder.\n  - `/tmp` may be full, so clone into `<dev>/.cache/` or `~/.cache/<unique-name>/`.\n  - When a server is needed, run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder to serve, parse the port it prints, and confirm the page is yours (fetch it and check for `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle`).\n  - Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill or killall, and never kill whatever holds a port.\n\n### 1.3 Ground rules that shape everything\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs: every feature in this plan ships complete. Where the plan offers a simpler fallback (§10), the fallback must be complete too.\n- All audio is synthesized in code: no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish (§5.13).\n- Controller first, for a TV seen from 10 feet away (§5.14 and §5.15). Keyboard and mouse must feel native. Touch-only devices get no touch controls (§5.15.6).\n- The game runs from `file://` with no network requests. The AI Hub client only talks to the hub when the hub opened the page.\n- The performance budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so FPS tuning is out of scope. Still avoid pathological designs, such as re-baking the floor every frame (§5.11).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game in brief\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genre | Arcade sports (cricket), over the top, with a comedy story campaign |\n| Perspective | 2.5D in the engine's projected-3D views. A **broadcast camera** (TV-style, behind the bowler, looking down the pitch at the batter) for every delivery. A high **wide camera** that follows the ball round the ground. A side-on **review camera** for third-umpire replays and a **close-up camera** for celebrations. Story scenes use the three-quarter view. |\n| Players | 1 to 4 on one machine: versus, co-op and 2v2, all on one shared screen. 1 to 4 on separate devices through AI Hub sessions (*Super Over Showdown*, parallel mode). |\n| Tone | Village cricket's gentle eccentricity: tea, cake, drizzle, the vicar as umpire, a cow at deep midwicket. Delivered in the voice of the cricket commentary box (digressive, plummy, dry, given to long tangents) and of Wisden-style match reports. Then turned up to arcade bombast: flaming bats, triple-ball bowling machines, sixes through the pub window. |\n| Session | Story campaign of about 40 minutes (5 chapters). A Quick Match takes 4 to 12 minutes. A Super Over Showdown takes 2 minutes. |\n\n**What the player can do:**\n- Bat: aim with the stick or mouse; play a ground, lofted or defensive shot, or leave; charge down the pitch; use a signature special.\n- Run: call runs, send the partner back, dive for the crease.\n- Bowl: pick 1 of 3 deliveries or a special; place the pitching marker; time the release meter; add aftertouch swing or drift; choose the field; bowl over or round the wicket.\n- Field: chase with the active fielder, dive, take catches on a timing ring, throw to either end for run-outs.\n- Review an umpire's decision once per innings.\n- Pick up arcade items, smash stadium targets, survive chaos events, go ON FIRE.\n\n**What the player can't do:** walk freely around the ground, edit teams or players, or play innings longer than 5 overs (no Test matches). In story mode the player always captains Kerfuffle CC. Each screen's help and the Nets mode make these limits clear.\n\n**Modes:** Story (*The Grand Kerfuffle*), Quick Match, Super Over Showdown (bat or bowl; high-score tables), Nets (practice, lessons and the \"Six Machine\" challenge), Versus (local 2–4), AI Hub session (parallel Super Over), plus Attract mode, Dev mode and About/Credits.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack\n- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2**, the full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, every module built in). Use these parts:\n  - `E.Game` scenes, boot steps, the standard pause menu, settings, controls, full screen and saves;\n  - `E.TileMap` for the ground;\n  - custom `E.View`s;\n  - `E.Humanoid` rigs, `E.Attack` specs and `E.ACTIONS`;\n  - `E.Body`, particles, lights and weather;\n  - `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`, `E.font`, `E.ui`;\n  - `game.script`, `game.tween`, `E.Path`;\n  - the `arcade` module (`E.Arcade` tables and initials), the `story` module (`E.Story` for cutscene data) and the `songs` module (jingles);\n  - the chip synth `game.audio`, plus our own Web Audio voice synth on the engine's audio context.\n- **AI Hub session client 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified.\n- **Node 18+** for the build and the headless tests. **Playwright 1.63.0** (pinned by `engine/package.json`) for the browser checks and the AI Hub harness.\n- Nothing else. No libraries, no CDN, no fetch.\n\n### 3.2 Folder layout (inside `<dev>`)\n```\nengine/                 copy of dist/handoff (modified: see 3.5 and engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md)\nvendor/aihub/           session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\nsrc/                    the game: plain script fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n  00-head.js            'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; shared helpers (clamp, lerp, ...)\n  01-const.js           units, geometry, enums (4)\n  02-tuning.js          TUNING registry: defaults, difficulty multipliers, live get/set, persistence (5.19)\n  03-rng.js             seeded streams: play, cosmetic, hub (3.4)\n  04-bus.js             event bus: on(type, fn), emit(type, data); a 200-entry event log for dev mode\n  05-store.js           profile: campaign progress, stars, unlocks, settings mirror, high-score ids\n  06-assets-store.js    the few store entries used, cut by engine/tools/asset-set.mjs (6.3)\n  07-assets-kerfuffle.js our new shapes and materials, as a standalone set file (6.5)\n  10-data-teams.js      teams and players (8.2)\n  11-data-grounds.js    ground recipes (8.3)\n  12-data-deliveries.js delivery catalogue (8.4)\n  13-data-shots.js      shot catalogue and the resolver table (8.5)\n  14-data-specials.js   signature specials (8.6)\n  15-data-arcade.js     items, stadium targets, chaos events (8.7)\n  16-data-story.js      chapters, scenes, beats, goals (8.8)\n  17-data-lines.js      commentary lines, match-report templates, UI copy (8.9)\n  18-data-music.js      songs and stings (8.10)\n  20-ball.js            ball physics and the trajectory predictor (5.1)\n  21-bowling.js         delivery choice, marker, meter, release, aftertouch (5.2)\n  22-batting.js         shot resolver, timing, contact, edges (5.3)\n  23-running.js         running between wickets (5.4)\n  24-fielding.js        fielders, interception, catches, throws, keeper (5.5)\n  25-umpire.js          decisions, LBW, run-out, stumping, review (5.6)\n  26-rules.js           match state, laws, scoring, scorecard (5.7)\n  27-ai.js              CPU batting, bowling, captain, fielding, autopilot (5.8)\n  28-hype.js            Hype meter, ON FIRE, specials runtime (5.9)\n  29-chaos.js           items, targets, chaos events, weather, time of day (5.10)\n  30-ground.js          TileMap builder from a ground recipe, floor texture, stands, crowd, panoramas (6.3)\n  31-cast.js            rig factory: kits, gear, bat, faces, portraits (6.4)\n  32-anim.js            pose library: stance, shots, bowling action, fielding, umpire signals (7)\n  33-fx.js              trails, flames, stumps and bails, splinters, fireworks, confetti (6.6)\n  34-camera.js          views and the camera director (5.11)\n  35-replay.js          recorder, Owl-Eye replay, third-umpire screen (5.11)\n  40-audio.js           SFX voices, crowd engine, music glue, stings (5.13)\n  41-voice.js           gibberish speech synth (5.13.3)\n  42-commentary.js      commentary director and captions (5.12)\n  43-glyphs.js          button and key icons, device-following prompts (5.14.3)\n  44-hud.js             match HUD (5.15.2)\n  45-ui.js              10-foot widgets: big menus, cards, panels, sliders (5.15)\n  50-title.js 51-attract.js 52-front.js (main menu, setup, versus lobby) 53-story.js (map, cutscenes)\n  54-match.js (gameplay scene) 55-results.js 56-nets.js 57-showdown.js 58-about.js\n  60-hub.js             AI Hub session flow (5.17)\n  70-dev.js             dev panel (5.19)\n  80-debug.js           game.debug: snapshot, force*, bot hooks (11.2)\n  99-start.js           new E.Game(...), scenes, boot steps, game.start\ntools/build.mjs         concatenate src -> dist/game.js; write dist/index.html (dev page) and the single-file game\ntools/serve.mjs         start python3 http.server on port 0 at 127.0.0.1; return { port, pid, stop() }\ntest/run.mjs            THE one test command (11.1)\ntest/suites/*.mjs       the suites\ntest/hub/               harness files + the built game, served for the harness run\ndist/                   game.js, index.html (loads ../engine/my-3d2dge.js and game.js: quick iteration)\nDESIGN-NOTES.md         short: how to add a team, a ground, a chapter, a delivery, a shot, a line\n```\n\n### 3.3 How the code is organized\n- **Simulation and presentation are separate.** The match simulation lives in `20–29`. It is pure and deterministic: fixed steps of 1/120 s, seeded RNG, no drawing and no audio. Presentation lives in `30–45`: it reads the simulation's state and listens on the event bus. Tests drive the simulation headless. The attract mode and the AI drive it through the same input interface a player uses.\n- **Registries.** All content is data keyed by id: teams, players, grounds, deliveries, shots, specials, items, targets, events, chapters and lines. Systems look things up by id, and content never edits system code. Adding a ground means a new recipe in `11-data-grounds.js` plus any new shapes. `DESIGN-NOTES.md` says how, for each kind of content.\n- **Event bus.** The rules emit events: `ball:released`, `ball:pitched`, `bat:contact {timing, shot}`, `ball:edge`, `ball:beaten`, `runs {n}`, `boundary {4|6}`, `wicket {how}`, `catch:dropped`, `appeal`, `decision {out, review}`, `extra {kind}`, `over:end`, `innings:end`, `match:end {result}`, `hype:full`, `fire:on|off`, `special {id}`, `item {id}`, `target:hit {id}`, `chaos {id}`, `rain:start|end`, `review:start|result`, `milestone {fifty|hattrick|maiden}`. Commentary, crowd, HUD, camera, Hype, achievements and the coverage tracker (§11) all subscribe.\n- **TUNING.** One registry of the live gameplay variables. Systems read `T('ballSpeed')` at each use and never cache it, so dev sliders take effect at once.\n- **The delivery director.** One state machine runs each ball (§5.7.3): `setup → runup → release → flight → inPlay → dead → between`. Each state has explicit exits and a timeout, so play can never stall.\n- **Scenes** (`game.go(name, data)`): `title`, `attract`, `front` (main menu, mode select, setup, versus lobby), `storymap`, `cutscene`, `match` (pausable), `results`, `nets` (pausable), `showdown` (pausable), `about`, `hubcard` and `hubround` (pausable). Story, Quick Match, Showdown, Nets and the attract mode all run inside the `match` scene machinery, using a `MatchConfig` (§5.7.1).\n\n### 3.4 Determinism and randomness\n- `RNG.play`: gameplay randomness (seam, AI errors, fielding). Seeded per match from `MatchConfig.seed`.\n- `RNG.cosmetic`: crowd, particles and commentary-line choice, so gameplay never depends on them.\n- In AI Hub sessions, the over's deliveries come from `AIHub.rng('over')` and each ball's fielding stream from `AIHub.rng('field' + n)`, so every copy faces identical balls whatever the player does (§5.17).\n- Never call `Math.random` in simulation code. It is fine for engine-internal cosmetics.\n\n### 3.5 Planned engine changes\nMake each change in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Log every one in `engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md`; About/Credits and the exit interview report them.\n1. **Cricket bat:** a new Humanoid `weapon: 'bat'`. Add it next to the `o.weapon === 'sword'` branch in the HD draw, which uses `ops.push({ d, f })` with `J.handR`, `J.bladeDir` and `o.bladeLen`. It is a flat willow blade: length `o.batLen` (default 15 units: 5 handle, 10 blade), width 1.7 units, a darker edge line, a toe, and a coloured sticker band. Grip in `colors.grip`, blade in `colors.willow`. Give it a two-handed grip: the left hand IK'd to the handle, 2.5 units above the right hand (`E.ik3` from the left shoulder), applied in the draw pose. Add a smear for swings.\n2. **Cricket gear:**\n   - `pads: true`: white leg guards over the shins and knees, with buckles in `colors.trim`;\n   - a `brim` hat style (umpire's and sun hats);\n   - `hat: { style: 'helmet', grille: true }`: a batting helmet with a face grille;\n   - `gloves: 'batting' | 'keeper'` for bigger hands;\n   - `face: { moustache: 'handlebar' | 'walrus' | null, beard: 'full' | 'goatee' | null, glasses: true }`.\n3. **Audio voice bus:** add `_voiceBus` beside `_sfxBus` and `_musBus`, a `voiceVolume` setting in the Sound group (label \"Voices\"), and `audio.bus(name)` returning the sfx, music or voice gain node, so the game's own Web Audio nodes follow the player's volumes.\n4. **Button symbols:** in the standard settings table (`glyphs`, around line 2999), set the default to `'playstation'` and the options to `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox (A B X Y)']]`.\n5. **Hub's Create + Options:** while `game.hubActive` is set and Pad8 (Create) is held, Pad9 (Options) must not open the pause menu.\n6. **Floor bakes:** `TileMap._floor` keeps at most 6 baked floors (it clears the cache past that). Raise the limit to 10, so the four match cameras and the story views never re-bake (§5.11).\n7. Any engine bug found along the way: fix it, and add one line about it to the log.\n\n### 3.6 Build and bundle (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { ... })();`, giving `dist/game.js`. Fail on a syntax error: run `new Function` on it in Node.\n2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `game.js` as separate scripts (fast iteration).\n3. Write the single file `../CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in exactly this order:\n   - `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`;\n   - **the session client** (`vendor/aihub/session.js`, byte-for-byte) as the first `<script>`;\n   - viewport meta, `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle</title>`;\n   - the hub tags (§5.17.1) and catalogue tags `hub:type`=`Game`, `hub:players`=`1-4`;\n   - the page CSS (from `engine/game/index.html`), `</head><body>`;\n   - the engine's boot splash markup (from `engine/game/index.html`, title CRICKET KERFUFFLE);\n   - `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>`;\n   - the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then `dist/game.js` inlined in a `<script>`.\n   Escape any `</script>` inside the inlined code.\n4. Print the size and the SHA-1 of the client block. The bundle test (§11) checks the block is identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Units, coordinates and constants (`01-const.js`)\n\n- **Engine convention:** 16 world units = 1 m (docs/ASSETS.md). x east (screen right), y south (screen down in yaw-0 views), z up.\n- **The pitch** runs along x = 0. The **striker's stumps are at y = 0** (the far end, top of the broadcast screen). The **bowler's stumps are at y = 320** (20 m; the near end, bottom of the screen). The striker's popping crease is at y = 20 and the bowler's at y = 300. Return creases are at x = ±21. The pitch strip is 48 units wide (3 m).\n- The camera always looks from behind the current bowler, so the bowling end is always at +y. At each change of ends, the batters swap logical places and the field is recomputed relative to the new striker. Visually, the \"far-end panorama\" (§6.3) switches to the other end's.\n- **Stumps:** physics box x ∈ [−3, 3], z ∈ [0, 12], depth 1. Drawn slightly larger for readability: 6 units wide, 12.5 tall, with two bails.\n- **Ball:** physics radius 0.6 units. Drawn as a 4×4 px sprite at minimum, with a 2 px shadow disc, a trail and a glow at night. Leather red `#b8202a` with a cream seam, white at night, pink at the Megadome.\n- **Players:** Humanoid size 1.15–1.25 (about 28–30 units tall), so `E.HEIGHTS` defaults hold.\n- **Batter's frame (handedness):** angles are measured from \"straight\" (toward the bowler, +y), positive toward the **off side**. A right-hander (RHB) faces −x in stance, so the off side is −x (screen left in the broadcast view). For a left-hander, everything mirrors (x → −x). Store `hand = +1` (RHB) or `−1` (LHB). The world direction for a batter-frame angle θ is `[−hand·sin θ, cos θ]`.\n- **Boundary:** an ellipse per ground (rx × ry, about 560–760 units, i.e. 35–48 m) centred on the pitch midpoint (0, 160), scaled by `boundaryScale` *(T)*.\n- **Physics constants:**\n\n  | Constant | Value |\n  |---|---|\n  | `gravity` *(T)* | 160 u/s² (real) |\n  | `ballSpeed` *(T)* | scale 0.62 on nominal pace (a \"140 km/h\" ball travels at 386 u/s and reaches the bat in about 0.75 s) |\n  | `pitchBounce` *(T)* | restitution 0.55 (times the ground's pitch factor) |\n  | Outfield rolling decay | exp(−k·dt), k = 0.55 (ground factor; wet ×1.6) |\n  | Grass bounce for hit balls | restitution 0.42 |\n  | `runSpeed` *(T)* | 120 u/s |\n  | `fieldSpeed` *(T)* | 105 u/s |\n  | `throwSpeed` *(T)* | 420 u/s |\n\n  At these values a single takes about 2.9 s, and a fielder 30 m away gathering in 1.2 s makes a close run-out.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Systems and mechanics\n\n### 5.1 Ball physics and predictor (`20-ball.js`)\n- **State:** `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, spin (rad/s about z, signed), swingA (lateral accel), age, phase: 'held'|'delivery'|'hit'|'rolling'|'dead', pitched, bounces, lastTouch: {kind, id}, carried (hit and not yet bounced), trail[] }`.\n- **Step (1/120 s), in order:**\n  1. Gravity: vz −= g·dt.\n  2. While `phase === 'delivery'` and not yet pitched, add swing: a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's ground velocity, `swingA · min(1, age/0.35)` (late swing).\n  3. Drift for spin.\n  4. Drag: v ×= (1 − 0.02·dt).\n  5. Integrate.\n  6. Ground contact at z ≤ 0:\n     - **On the pitch** (|x| ≤ 24 and y in −20..340), for a delivery: vz' = −vz · pitchBounce · ground.bounce · delivery.bounce. Horizontal speed × (0.86 − spinGrip). Turn the ground velocity by `turn° · ground.turn · T('spin')` plus the ball's stored seam deviation `ball.seamDev`. That deviation is drawn once **at release** from RNG.play (normal, σ = `seam` *(T)* · ground.seam · delivery.seam degrees) and kept on the ball. Emit `ball:pitched`.\n     - **On grass** (any ball after the bat): bounce at 0.42; below 25 u/s vertically it becomes `rolling`, with decay k.\n  7. Boundary check (§5.7.4).\n  8. Target and item checks (§5.10).\n  9. Stumps check (§5.6).\n- **Solving a delivery** (`solveDelivery(release, target, kmh, deliveryType)`): given the release point (x = ±8 for over or round the wicket, y = 300, z = 34), the pitch target (tx, ty) and the speed, find the initial velocity that lands at the target *without* swing, using `E.arc`-style closed-form projectile maths with the delivery's dip. Swing and aftertouch then move the real pitching point. That is the point of swing, and what the batter must read.\n- **Predictor** (`predict(ballState, seconds, opts)`): runs the same step function on a copy and returns sampled points `{t, x, y, z, phase}` every 1/120 s. It is used for:\n  - the batting timing (when the ball reaches the contact plane);\n  - the AI's reads;\n  - fielder interception;\n  - the landing point of a catch;\n  - LBW (the path continued past the pad);\n  - the Owl-Eye tube.\n\n  The step function draws no random numbers (the seam deviation is already on the ball), so the prediction equals reality exactly. The AI adds its own perception error on top.\n- **Tests (unit-ball):**\n  - 50 random solved deliveries without swing pitch within 1 unit of the target;\n  - an outswinger to a right-hander lands on the off side of the target (−x) and an inswinger on the leg side;\n  - a leg break pitched on middle turns toward −x for a right-hander;\n  - the predictor matches the stepped simulation within 0.01 units over 3 s;\n  - rolling balls stop;\n  - a ball dropped from z = 50 bounces lower each time.\n\n### 5.2 Bowling (`21-bowling.js`)\n**The flow for a human bowler:**\n1. **Between balls**:\n   - R1 (Space) cycles the field preset (Attacking, Balanced, Defensive, or Spin attack for spinners);\n   - L1 (Shift) switches over or round the wicket;\n   - the stick, WASD or the mouse moves the **pitch marker**: a ring on the pitch, clamped to y ∈ [20, 230] and |x| ≤ 30, with labelled length bands *yorker / full / good / short / bouncer* drawn faintly on the pitch the first few overs;\n   - the delivery panel at the bottom shows the bowler's 3 deliveries on ✕ □ ○ and the special on △ (greyed out until Hype is full).\n2. Pressing a delivery button starts the **run-up**: pace bowlers run 2.0 s, medium 1.5 s, spinners 0.9 s. The marker can still move during the run-up, at half speed.\n3. **The release meter**: during the last 0.7 s, a needle sweeps a bar left → right → left at `meterSpeed` *(T)* (1.4 Hz). The centre zone (width `meterSweet` *(T)* 0.18 of the bar, scaled by bowler skill) is green. Press any delivery button or click (✕/J/LMB) to release:\n   - **green**: accuracy scatter ±2 units, pace and spin 100–105%;\n   - **amber**: scatter ±10, 90%;\n   - **red edge**: scatter ±18, 80%;\n   - **the far-right red tip** (the last 6%) is an **overstep**: a no-ball and a FREE HIT;\n   - **no press**: auto-release at the end with an amber result.\n4. **Aftertouch**: for 0.25 s after release, the stick's horizontal push adds swing (seamers) or drift (spinners), up to the delivery's allowance ±0.6. The mouse's horizontal movement does the same.\n5. **Specials** (△ when Hype is full) skip the meter (always green) and add their effect (§8.6).\n\n**CPU bowlers** use the same pipeline: the AI chooses a delivery and a target (§5.8), its meter press lands at a skill-based offset, and the result goes through the same code.\n\n**Delivery types** are data (§8.4): pace, swing, spin and medium families, each with nominal km/h, length band limits (a bouncer pitches ≥ 140 units from the batter's crease), swing, seam, turn, bounce and dip multipliers, FX and AI weights. The HUD shows the speed after release (\"143 KM/H\") and the delivery name in small type.\n\n**Wides and no-balls:**\n- A **wide** is a ball passing the striker's popping crease more than 26 units to the off side or more than 18 to the leg side of the middle-stump line (x = 0, measured in the striker's frame), without touching the bat or the batter. A ball passing above z = 40 (a head-high bouncer) is also a wide.\n- A **no-ball** comes from an overstep, or a full toss above z = 26 at the crease (\"a beamer\": over the top, the commentators gasp).\n\n**Tests:**\n- meter green → pitches within 3 units of the marker;\n- red tip → `extra:noBall` and the next ball is `freeHit`;\n- no input → amber auto-release;\n- aftertouch right → pitch point moves +x;\n- run-up durations per family;\n- a field-preset cycle changes the fielder targets;\n- over/round moves the release x.\n\n### 5.3 Batting (`22-batting.js`)\n- **Stance:** the striker takes guard with the body 8 units to the leg side of the stumps line, side-on, the bat grounded and the head turned to the bowler (§7). The non-striker backs up at the bowler's end.\n- **Inputs:**\n  - **aim** (the stick, WASD, or the mouse's ground point relative to the batter via `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse wins while it moved in the last second, else the stick). Aim is **in screen space**: push toward where the ball should go as you see it, turned into a ground direction with `game.view.screenDirToGround`. Down is straight back past the bowler, left and right go across the field, up goes behind the batter (fine). An aim arrow on the grass at the batter's feet shows the direction live;\n  - **shot** ✕/J/LMB (ground), **loft** □/K/RMB (in the air), **block** ○/L/Q (defend);\n  - **special** △/I/F;\n  - **charge** R2/E: before the ball pitches, step 24 units down the pitch. This turns a good length into a half-volley, but a miss off a spinner means a stumping chance.\n  - No button means a **leave**: the bat goes up. Against a bouncer above z = 28 the batter ducks or sways by himself when leaving or blocking.\n- **Timing model:** each shot in the catalogue has `contactAt` (seconds from press to the bat's contact moment, e.g. a drive 0.13 s, a cut 0.11, a block 0.08). The predictor gives `tArrive`, when the ball reaches the shot's contact plane (front-foot shots y ≈ 36, back-foot y ≈ 14; a charge moves it). With Δ = (tPress + contactAt) − tArrive:\n\n  | Grade | Window | Result |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | PERFECT | \\|Δ\\| ≤ `perfectMs` *(T)* 40 ms | full power, true direction |\n  | GOOD | ≤ `goodMs` *(T)* 85 ms | 85% power, ±6° error |\n  | EARLY / LATE | ≤ `okMs` *(T)* 150 ms | 60% power; direction pulled (early: toward leg) or sliced (late: toward off / behind), launch +8° (skier risk) |\n  | MISS | beyond 150 ms | beaten: the ball goes on to pads, stumps or keeper |\n\n  Presses made after the ball passed the contact plane are ignored. Show a big timing word for 0.6 s (PERFECT in gold, GOOD, EARLY, LATE), on by default.\n- **Shot resolver:** maps (button, stick sector in the batter's frame, ball length at pitch, ball line and height at contact) to one shot from the catalogue (§8.5). For example: ✕ + off sector + full ball = cover drive; ✕ + leg + short = pull; □ + leg + short = hook; □ + behind = scoop; □ + straight vs a yorker = helicopter. A neutral stick uses the shot's natural direction for that ball.\n- **Reach check:** each shot has a contact zone (line range and height range relative to the batter). Outside the zone by up to `edge` *(T)* × 4 units gives an **edge**:\n  - a thin edge goes to the keeper or slips: speed 35–55% of the incoming, deflected 150–175°, slight rise;\n  - a thick edge goes to third man or gully;\n  - an inside edge goes onto the pad or stumps (\"dragged on\").\n\n  Further outside is a **miss**.\n- **The outgoing ball:**\n  - direction: the aim direction, else the shot's natural direction, plus the timing error, plus RNG.play noise of σ 2°;\n  - speed: `shot.speed × batter.power × timingFactor × (1 + 0.25·incoming/386) × T('batPower' or 'loftPower')`, × 1.2 when ON FIRE;\n  - launch angle: from the shot (ground 2–10°, lofted 28–48°), plus timing (early lofted shots go higher).\n\n  Ground shots bounce and roll. Lofted shots carry.\n- **Visual alignment:** the swing's animated contact is timed to `contactAt` through `E.Attack` phases (the wind phase ends at contact). During the last 30 ms, the ball's *drawn* position is pulled toward the bat's sweet spot, cosmetically only. Contact spawns splinters or sparks at the bat and plays the \"tock\".\n- **Tests (unit-shots, sim):**\n  - every resolver row gives its shot;\n  - Δ = 0 gives PERFECT and Δ = 60 ms gives GOOD;\n  - a press 200 ms early misses;\n  - a ball 6 units outside a drive's zone edges behind;\n  - stick straight plus PERFECT ✕ on a half-volley gives a direction within ±10° of straight;\n  - a charge then a miss vs spin with the keeper up gives a stumping;\n  - leaving a ball on the stumps gets the batter bowled;\n  - leaving a ball 20 units outside off is safe;\n  - ducking a bouncer leaves the batter not out with no wide called unless z > 40.\n\n### 5.4 Running between wickets (`23-running.js`)\n- **Calls:** after contact (or byes and leg byes when the ball beats the bat and gets past the keeper), the striker's call is shown in a speech bubble: **YES / NO / WAIT**, with a glyph prompt \"R1 RUN\" or \"L1 STAY\". The human presses **R1/Space** to run, **again** at the crease to turn for another run, and **L1/Shift** to send both back. **R1 within 50 units of the crease** while a throw is coming in makes the batter **dive**: the bat slides the last 20 units, so the bat is grounded 0.15 s sooner, and he gets up after 0.6 s.\n- **The partner:** the non-striker is the AI (1P), or the second human in co-op (§5.16.5). The AI partner follows the human's YES and NO. In co-op both must call YES within 0.4 s, or there's a **mix-up**: both may run to the same end, and whoever left his crease is the one run out. The two gibberish shouts overlap.\n- **Movement:** batters accelerate at 600 u/s² to `runSpeed` *(T)*, turn at the crease in 0.25 s, carry the bat, and slow 15% on sand or wet ground. Runs count as each batter grounds the bat beyond the far popping crease; a short run is not modelled.\n- **AI calling** (also used for the YES / NO / WAIT bubble hint): compare the batters' time to complete the next run with the fielding side's earliest time to break the stumps at the far end (§5.5 interception plus throw time). Margin > 0.35 s gives YES. Margin < −0.15 s gives NO. In between it's WAIT, and the CPU decides by aggression.\n- **Tests:** a push to cover with R1 once gives 1 run and the strike rotates; R1 twice on a slow outfield shot gives 2; L1 mid-run sends both back to their crease; a co-op mix-up gives exactly one run-out; a dive beats a throw arriving 0.1 s after the undived time.\n\n### 5.5 Fielding (`24-fielding.js`)\n- **Positions:** field presets are lists of `{ pos: 'slip1', θ, d }` in the batter's frame (§8.4.2), so a left-hander gets the field mirrored. Between balls, fielders walk to their spots (and teleport if more than 3 s away). As the bowler runs in they **walk in** a few steps, crouched (the engine pose `crouch`, with forward motion).\n- **Interception:** on contact, the predictor samples the ball every 0.05 s. For each fielder, find the earliest t where dist(fielder, ball(t)) ≤ fieldSpeed·(t − reaction 0.18 s) + reach (catch reach 9 for aerial balls at z ∈ [4, 40]; stop reach 6 on the ground; dive +18).\n  - The best fielder **chases**, the second-best **backs up**, the keeper and bowler run to their stumps, and the rest hold.\n  - If no interception exists before the boundary, the nearest deep fielder chases anyway (it reads well) and a FOUR or SIX follows.\n- **Catches** (the ball carried from the bat):\n  - **CPU side:** success p = clamp(fielder.catch × difficulty − 0.25·(ball speed/400) − 0.3·(dive ? 1 : 0), 0.05, 0.97), drawn from RNG.play.\n  - **Human side** (Manual or Assisted fielding, §5.15.5): the **catch ring** QTE. An outer ring shrinks onto an inner ring at the catch point over `catchRing` *(T)* 0.9 s. ✕/J/LMB within `catchWindow` *(T)* ±110 ms of the overlap catches, and a PERFECT press with a dive becomes a \"SCREAMER!\" with slow-mo. Early or late is a juggle (50% then a 2nd ring of 0.4 s). No press or a miss is DROPPED. In Assisted mode, easy chances (p > 0.85) are caught automatically.\n- **Ground fielding:** the fielder gathers when the ball is within reach: 0.25 s for a stop, a dive stop on ✕ if the ball passes within 18 units. A misfield chance (1 − fielder.ground) × 0.1 lets the ball through.\n- **Throws:**\n  - **Human:** after gathering, **✕/J/LMB throws to the striker's end** (the keeper's end), **□/K/RMB to the bowler's end**, **○/L/Q lobs back to the keeper** (no shy). With the mouse, clicking either set of stumps in the world (or its icon) throws there. A press within 0.3 s of gathering is a \"rocket\": throw speed +20%.\n  - **CPU:** throws to the end with the better run-out margin.\n  - The throw follows an `E.arc` toward the stumps with error N(0, `throwError` *(T)* × distance/480). A **direct hit** is when the path crosses the stumps box. Otherwise the keeper or bowler, if within 10 units of the stumps, collects and breaks them in 0.2 s. A missed shy with nobody backing up gives **overthrows**: the ball runs on, and the batters may run more.\n- **Keeper:** stands 160 units back to pace and 24 units (standing up) to spin and medium. Catches edges (CPU p from keeper skill; human edges use the catch ring too), collects deliveries, stumps after a charge-and-miss (0.25 s), and receives throws.\n- **Tests:**\n  - an interception is found for a ball hit straight at mid-off;\n  - nobody intercepts a perfectly placed cover drive into a gap at 450 u/s on a fast outfield → FOUR;\n  - a CPU catch with p = 0.97 under a seeded RNG gives \"caught\";\n  - a human ring press at the overlap catches, and no press drops;\n  - a ✕ throw with the striker 30 units short and a direct hit is OUT;\n  - a missed shy with no backup gives ≥ 1 overthrow;\n  - no fielder is ever more than 15 s in one state (stuck check, run in bot-match).\n\n### 5.6 Umpiring and dismissals (`25-umpire.js`)\n- **Bowled:** the ball enters the stumps box with bails on (the stumps check in the ball step); the bails fly as physics sprites. **Hit wicket:** a back-foot shot with an EARLY/LATE grade has a 1.5% chance; the bat clips the stumps, comically. **Caught:** §5.5. **Run out:** stumps broken while the batter heading to that end hasn't grounded his bat. **Stumped:** the keeper breaks the stumps while the striker is out of his ground after a miss, and not attempting a run.\n- **LBW:** if the ball meets the front pad box before the bat (box around the leading leg: 6×4×16), evaluate the truth:\n  1. pitched outside leg stump → not out;\n  2. impact outside off stump *and* a shot was offered → not out;\n  3. the predictor continues from the impact with the pad removed: does it hit the stumps box (inflated by the ball radius)? → out.\n\n  If the predicted path is within 1 unit of the stumps' edge, the result is **UMPIRE'S CALL**: the on-field decision stands on review.\n  - **The on-field decision:** the umpire is \"right\" with probability `umpire.accuracy` (Rev. Hollis 0.86, others 0.92), from RNG.play. Wrong decisions are what reviews are for.\n- **Appeals:** the fielding side appeals (\"HOWZAT!\" in gibberish, arms up) for every LBW candidate, caught-behind edge and close run-out. Umpire signals follow (§7).\n- **The Kerfuffle Review System (KRS):** each side has 1 review per innings. After a decision, the batting side (on OUT) or the fielding side (on NOT OUT) has 4 s to press **△/I/F \"REVIEW\"** (the CPU reviews when its truth confidence > 0.7). The review plays the **Owl-Eye** replay (§5.11.4): the ball path tube, the pitching and impact markers, a three-light verdict (PITCHING, IMPACT, WICKETS), each green, red or amber for umpire's call. Then OUT or NOT OUT is stamped like a slot machine. A successful review is kept; a failed one is lost.\n- **Third umpire** (automatic, no review used): run-outs and stumpings with a margin under 0.15 s, and catches where the ball touched the ground within 0.05 s of the catch, are sent upstairs. The side-on review camera replays in slow-mo and a big screen reveals OUT or NOT OUT.\n- **Tests (unit-umpire):** the three LBW truth rules; umpire's call; the accuracy draw is seeded; a review overturns a wrong decision and is kept; a failed review is lost; a run-out margin of 0.1 s triggers the third umpire; a stumping requires the striker out of his ground.\n\n### 5.7 Match rules and scoring (`26-rules.js`)\n\n#### 5.7.1 MatchConfig and state\n```js\nMatchConfig = { mode: 'story'|'quick'|'showdown'|'nets'|'attract'|'hub'|'versus',\n  teams: ['ker','mud'], ground: 'green', overs: 2, wickets: 5, ballsPerOver: 6, maxOversPerBowler: 'auto',\n  difficulty: 'village'|'county'|'test'|'kerfuffle', kerfuffleRules: true, timeOfDay: 'day'|'dusk'|'night',\n  weather: 'clear'|'overcast'|'rain'|'fog', toss: true, scenario: null | { innings, target, battingFirst, preset },\n  humans: { bat: [inputIndex...], bowl: [...] }, seed, superOver: true, reviewsPerInnings: 1, chapter: null }\n```\n- `maxOversPerBowler: 'auto'` means ceil(overs / 5): one over each for 1–5-over innings, so several bowler types appear.\n- The match state holds the innings array: runs, wickets, legal balls, extras `{wd, nb, b, lb}`, batter cards (runs, balls, 4s, 6s, how out), bowler cards (balls, runs, wickets, maidens), ball records, partnership, target, revised target, free-hit flag, reviews left, Hype, ON FIRE, items held, and the result.\n- `rules.applyBall(state, outcome) → { state, events[] }` is a **pure function**. Every ball record is kept for the results screen, the highlights and the report.\n\n#### 5.7.2 The laws (arcade cut)\n- An over is 6 legal balls. Wides and no-balls add 1 run and are re-bowled.\n- **FREE HIT** after a no-ball: the only dismissal is run out. The umpire circles his finger and the HUD flashes FREE HIT.\n- The strike changes on odd runs and at the end of each over.\n- An innings ends at the overs limit, the wickets limit, or (2nd innings) when the target is passed.\n- **Result:** won by N runs (side batting first) or by N wickets (chasing side), with \"B balls to spare\" text. A **tie** goes to a **Super Over**: each side bats 1 over with 2 wickets, the side batting second in the match bats first, and if it's tied again the side with more boundaries in the Super Over wins. This is the real rule simplified, and it is shown as a tooltip.\n- Byes and leg byes come from running after a miss (leg bye if it hit the pad).\n- Milestones: fifty (rare in short games, but supported), hat-trick (three wickets in three of a bowler's balls) and maiden.\n\n#### 5.7.3 The delivery director (per ball; every state has a timeout)\n\n| State | Contents | Exit |\n|---|---|---|\n| `setup` | fielders to positions, batter guard, bowler at the mark; human chooses delivery/marker or waits; HUD prompts; CPU bowler \"thinks\" 0.6 s | the bowler starts the run-up (human press, or CPU after 0.6–1.2 s); 20 s idle in Quick Match shows a nudge prompt |\n| `runup` | bowler runs; marker; meter; charge allowed | release |\n| `release`/`flight` | ball spawned; aftertouch 0.25 s; batter swing window | contact, miss, leave, or the ball reaching y < −30 |\n| `inPlay` | fielders chase; batters run; throws; catches; boundary | dead-ball condition |\n| `dead` | umpire signal; appeals; decision; review window; replay (if enabled) | done, or 12 s cap |\n| `between` | score update, events, commentary, over end, bowler select (human: big portrait picker), innings end | next `setup` |\n\n**Dead-ball conditions:** the ball crosses the boundary; a catch is completed; bowled; a run-out or stumping is completed; the ball is settled (in the keeper's or bowler's hands, or a fielder within 32 units of the stumps holding it, with both batters grounded, for 0.8 s); or a hard cap of 15 s in `inPlay` (a fielder fetches it and the umpire calls dead).\n\n**Pacing:** between balls takes ≤ 2.5 s excluding replays. Replays and celebrations are skippable with ✕/J/LMB.\n\n#### 5.7.4 Boundaries\nWhen the ball crosses the boundary ellipse: if it touched the ground since leaving the bat (or was rolling), it's FOUR; if it carried over on the full, SIX. A ball hit over the boundary keeps flying for the show: into the stands (crowd scatter, a spectator catch \"Crowd catch!\", a cheer), into a stadium target (§5.10), or into the sea or pub.\n\n#### 5.7.5 The Kerfuffle Method (rain rule; Dustbowl Downs, and a dev-mode trigger)\nWhen rain cuts the overs of the 2nd innings: resources R(o, w) = (o / O) · (1 − 0.07·w), where o = overs left, O = overs at the start of the innings and w = wickets lost. Revised target = floor(firstInningsRuns × R_after / R_before) + 1, never less than the current score + 1. It is shown on a chalkboard card by Mrs Crumb with absurd working (\"carry the scone\") and a big new target. Test it with fixed numbers.\n\n#### 5.7.6 Tests (unit-rules)\nLegal ball counting with wides and no-balls; free hit; strike rotation on 1, 2 and 3 runs and at over end; each innings-end condition; result texts; the Super Over trigger and its tie-break; the Kerfuffle Method numbers; max overs per bowler; the scorecard totals equal the sums of the ball records.\n\n### 5.8 AI (`27-ai.js`)","numLines":420,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1435}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013DRJg85aQzZLNChZj5jonj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
