{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghy1eevdWamDQt44uq9z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018vBn14FEaLfW4qMYVGhtg2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","offset":420,"limit":420},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18798,"cache_read_input_tokens":25161,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18798},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"9e0432f9-7b7a-488d-bfc6-4e3fde93a404","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:28:13.834Z","request_id":"req_011Cghy1eRnhYFPqA1Ueb8z4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018vBn14FEaLfW4qMYVGhtg2":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","offset":420,"limit":420}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018vBn14FEaLfW4qMYVGhtg2","type":"tool_result","content":"420\t### 5.8 AI (`27-ai.js`)\n421\t- **Difficulty** multiplies TUNING (§5.19.2): Village, County, Test, Kerfuffle!.\n422\t- **CPU batting:**\n423\t  1. Read the ball: the predictor, plus a perception error σ = `aiBatSkill` *(T)* 55 ms × (1.3 vs mystery or special balls, 0.8 vs full tosses) × batter technique.\n424\t  2. Choose the intent from the match situation: required rate vs current rate, wickets in hand, balls left, plus `aiAggression` *(T)* 0.5 and the batter's style (slogger, accumulator, blocker). Ball type: full and straight → drive or loft; short → pull or hook (or duck if hook-shy); wide outside off → cut or leave; good length on the stumps → defend unless aggressive; yorker → block or helicopter if a slogger.\n425\t  3. Choose a direction: the largest angular gap between fielders within 25 units for ground shots; for lofted shots, the direction with the fewest deep fielders, toward the shorter boundary.\n426\t  4. Press at the predicted time + the error. Run calls as in §5.4.\n427\t- **CPU bowling:** a per-batter plan from the bowler's type, weighted by the batter's weaknesses (data: `weak: ['short','spin']`). Pace: mostly good length on off stump, a bouncer every ~5, a yorker at the death. Swing: an outswinger set-up then an inswinger. Spin: flighted outside off, then a googly or doosra. The target scatter is `aiBowlAccuracy` *(T)* 10 units × the meter result (offsets drawn by skill). Use the special when Hype is full in the last over or vs a set batter.\n428\t- **CPU captain:** picks the next bowler (one not over the limit, preferring a type change and a bowler who suits the ground), picks a field preset by situation (attacking when defending a big total or vs a new batter; defensive at the death), and chooses bat or bowl at the toss (bat on a flat pitch, bowl when overcast).\n429\t- **CPU fielding:** §5.5.\n430\t- **Autopilot** (`ai.autopilot(side, role, { cheat })`) produces **virtual input** through `input.hold/tap` exactly as a player's would. It powers the bots in tests, the dev toggles Auto-bat and Auto-bowl, and the attract mode. `cheat` options: perfect timing, a chosen shot or outcome, a guaranteed catch, never out.\n431\t- **Tests:** the bot-match suite (CPU vs CPU, each ground, plausible statistics, §11.3); the AI never presses after the contact plane; an aggressive chaser with 20 needed off 6 lofts more than 50% of balls; a defensive AI at 0 required leaves or blocks.\n432\t\n433\t### 5.9 Hype, ON FIRE and specials (`28-hype.js`)\n434\t\n435\t**The Kerfuffle meter (Hype)** belongs to each team, 0–100, and is shown at the bottom centre. Gains are × `hypeGain` *(T)*.\n436\t\n437\t| Gain | Batting | Bowling or fielding |\n438\t|---|---|---|\n439\t| Large | six +25, stadium target +30 | wicket +30, maiden +20 |\n440\t| Medium | four +15, three runs +10 | screamer catch +15, direct hit +15 |\n441\t| Small | PERFECT +8 | beaten the bat +8, dot ball +6 |\n442\t| Any side | chaos crowd moments +5 | |\n443\t\n444\tAt 100, △ \"SPECIAL READY\" pulses and the crowd chants \"KER-FUF-FLE!\". Using the special empties the meter.\n445\t- **Specials** belong to characters (§8.6): the batting special uses the current striker's, the bowling special the current bowler's. A player without one uses the generic *Kerfuffle Shot* (a guaranteed PERFECT lofted hit, fire trail) or *Kerfuffle Ball* (a green-meter delivery with +15% pace and double swing).\n446\t- **ON FIRE:**\n447\t  - **Batter:** `fireStreak` *(T)* 3 consecutive boundaries. The bat burns with flame particles and the music brings in its `fire` layer (`audio.layer('fire', 1)`, §5.13.4). Timing windows ×1.5 and shot power ×1.2. It ends on a dot ball or a wicket, or after 6 balls.\n448\t  - **Bowler:** 3 consecutive dot balls or wickets. The ball burns with a trail: pace +10%, edge zone ×1.4. It ends when a boundary is conceded, or after 6 balls.\n449\t  - Commentary and crowd go wild, with a sting and a screen flash (respecting the Comfort settings).\n450\t- **Tests:** three boundaries give `fire:on` and widened windows; a dot ball gives `fire:off`; at Hype 100, △ consumes it and emits `special`; each special's effect flag is observable in the snapshot (e.g. `ball.fx === 'vanish'` during its invisible window).\n451\t\n452\t### 5.10 Items, stadium targets, chaos events, weather and time of day (`29-chaos.js`)\n453\t- **Kerfuffle rules** (a match setting, on in story from chapter 2, default on in Quick Match) enable items and specials. Off gives \"purist arcade\": no items, no specials, no chaos (so players can see what is core and what is extra).\n454\t- **Items** (§8.7.1):\n455\t  - At the start of each over, with probability 0.35 × `chaosRate` *(T)* per ground, a batting item token floats over a random outfield spot (an RNG.play seed). The ball passing through its 14-unit sphere (in the air or along the ground) gives it to the batting side.\n456\t  - Bowling items are earned by 2 dot balls in an over.\n457\t  - Items **auto-apply** on their next relevant event. No extra buttons: the HUD shows a held item icon labelled \"ARMED\".\n458\t- **Stadium targets** (§8.3): each ground has 3–4 landmark boxes in world space (a sphere or box per target). A ball whose flight passes through one triggers it: bonus runs added to the batting score as \"KERFUFFLE BONUS +N\", Hype +30, slow-mo 0.4 s, particles, crowd and commentary, plus a target state (a broken window sprite, a spinning weathervane, a jumbotron glitch). It resets each match. Bonus runs count toward totals and targets, and the scorecard lists them as \"Bonus\".\n459\t- **Chaos events** (§8.7.3) are scripted mini-events with a per-ground pool, firing between balls (never mid-delivery) at a per-over chance × `chaosRate` *(T)*. Examples: Doris the cow wanders onto the outfield and blocks a fielding lane for 2 overs (fielders path round her); seagulls steal a ball stopped in the outfield (byes); bees chase a fielder (−30% speed for 1 over); the tide comes in; the rain delay; dust devils; ghost fielders phasing; Thrumpton's advert break.\n460\t- **Weather and time of day:**\n461\t  - `game.weather` for rain (the delay) and dust or leaves (cosmetic).\n462\t  - **Fog** is our own screen overlay: dithered translucency (`px.blend`) whose strength grows with distance from the camera focus. Fielders beyond 400 units fade, and the ball is only visible within 260 units of the batter unless lit. It is drawn after the world and before the HUD.\n463\t  - **Overcast:** swing ×1.5, ambient 0.78 with a blue-grey tint.\n464\t  - **Day:** lights enabled with ambient 0.92, plus moving cloud-shadow decals.\n465\t  - **Dusk:** ambient 0.7, warm.\n466\t  - **Night:** ambient 0.42; four floodlight pylons each add `lights.add(x, y, 220, 1100, .75, { color: '#fff4dc' })`; the ball is drawn `emissive`.\n467\t- **Tests:** a forced item spawn on the ball's predicted path is collected and applied; a target hit adds its bonus and emits `target:hit`; each chaos event can be forced (dev and test) and resolves within its time without errors; overcast doubles the measured swing displacement vs clear (allowing for the 1.5 factor); night enables 4 lights.\n468\t\n469\t### 5.11 Camera director and replays (`34-camera.js`, `35-replay.js`)\n470\t\n471\t#### 5.11.1 Views\n472\tFour custom orthographic views, made once. Never tween zoom in a match: each new zoom re-bakes the TileMap floor, because `_floor` caches by view id, yaw, pitch and scale. So use fixed views and cut between them, like TV.\n473\t\n474\t| View | `new E.View(...)` | Use |\n475\t|---|---|---|\n476\t| broadcast | `('broadcast','Broadcast', 0, T('camPitch') 12, T('camScale') 1.45, 1)` | every delivery: bowler at the bottom, pitch, batter, keeper and slips at the top, the far-end panorama behind them |\n477\t| wide | `('wide','Wide', 0, 55, T('wideScale') 0.8, 1)` | ball in play round the ground |\n478\t| review | `('review','Side-on', 90, 8, 2.4, 1)` | third umpire and run-out replays: the crease seen side-on |\n479\t| close | `('close','Close-up', 0, 22, 2.6, 1)` | wickets, celebrations, umpire signals, specials' wind-ups |\n480\t\n481\t- Scenes set `camera: { zoom: false }` (the director frames shots; no player zoom). `game.setView(view)` cuts, and the engine snaps the camera on a view change.\n482\t- **Why pitch 12° and scale 1.45:** a strongly foreshortened, telephoto TV look. A Humanoid about 29 units tall draws 29 × cos 12° × 1.45 ≈ 41 px. The 300 units from the bowler's crease to the striker's crease take 300 × sin 12° × 1.45 ≈ 90 px. On a 240-px-tall screen that places the striker's feet at about 47% of the height and the bowler's crease at about 85%. The keeper (y −160, 48 px above the striker's feet), the slips and the far-end panorama fill the top, and the bowler runs into view from below the bottom edge. In M1, check that the TileMap floor bakes correctly at this low pitch; if it doesn't, use 15° and scale 1.35.\n483\t- **Warm the floor bakes** in a boot step (\"Mowing the outfield\"): draw one frame in each view with `game.advance(0, { draw: true })`, or by calling `map.drawFloor` on an offscreen renderer once per view. The first cut then never hitches.\n484\t\n485\t#### 5.11.2 The director\n486\t- A small state machine of named shots: `broadcastSetup`, `broadcastBall`, `wideFollow`, `boundaryCrowd`, `wicketClose`, `catchFollow`, `reviewSideOn`, `celebration`, `overCard`.\n487\t- **The broadcast framing:** choose the focus point each frame so the striker's feet sit at 47% of `r.H` and the bowler's popping crease at about 85%, on any aspect ratio. A taller screen shows more panorama above and more run-up below; a wider one shows more of the off and leg sides.\n488\t- **Cut rules:**\n489\t  - Cut to **wide** 0.12 s after contact when the predicted ball path leaves the broadcast frame within 0.8 s (the screen's edges, or north of y −185, behind the panorama, §6.3), or the shot is lofted.\n490\t  - Stay **broadcast** for blocks, leaves, misses, edges to the keeper and slips, and stumpings.\n491\t  - In **wide**, `game.focus` leads the ball: the point between the ball and its predicted landing or interception point, with `cam.smooth = 0.22`. At the boundary, hold on the crowd for 0.8 s.\n492\t  - For a **wicket**, cut to close on the stumps or catcher at timeScale 0.35 for 0.8 s, then to the umpire's signal.\n493\t  - A **review** runs on the review cam (§5.11.4).\n494\t  - **Between balls**, hard-cut back to broadcast. Every camera change inside a match is a hard cut; the engine's fades (`game.go(..., { fade })`) are only for scene changes.\n495\t- **Screen shake and flash** respect the Comfort settings (`game.shake`, `game.flash`).\n496\t\n497\t#### 5.11.3 The recorder\n498\t- A ring buffer of the last 10 s at 60 Hz: each actor's rig input state (the object passed to `rig.update`), the ball state, the bails and particles' seeds, and the HUD values.\n499\t- The **instant replay** after big moments (six, wicket, screamer, target smash, special) re-poses the rigs from the recorded states on the broadcast cam (or the cam that suits the moment), at 0.5× speed with a \"REPLAY\" bug and the ball's trail line. Replay frequency is a setting (Always / Big moments / Never); any button skips.\n500\t- **Highlights reel:** the results screen keeps the recorded windows of the match's top 3 moments (scored by event weight), up to 3 × 6 s, in memory only.\n501\t\n502\t#### 5.11.4 Owl-Eye and the third umpire\n503\t- **Owl-Eye** (LBW reviews, and the dev toggle \"Show trajectory\"): the ball path drawn as a translucent tube (`px.polyDither` segments) from release to impact, then the predicted continuation in a different colour to the stumps. Pitching and impact markers are discs on the pitch. Then a three-light verdict panel and the stamp.\n504\t- **Third-umpire screen:** the review cam replays the crease at 0.25×, freezes at the moment the bails break, and zooms a \"TV\" frame overlay. A big OUT or NOT OUT reveal ticks between them like a slot reel for 1.2 s, then lands with crowd and commentary.\n505\t\n506\t#### 5.11.5 Tests\n507\tThe director picks wide for a lofted drive and broadcast for a block; a review sequence completes and returns to `between`; the recorder replays a recorded six with the same ball positions within 0.5 units; the dev and test flag `warmViews` covers 4 bakes; bakes stay ≤ 10 entries during a full match (instrument `map.floors` size).\n508\t\n509\t### 5.12 Commentary and narrative text (`42-commentary.js`, `17-data-lines.js`)\n510\t- **The commentators**:\n511\t  - **Sir Aubrey Fennimore-Wolde**: plummy, unhurried, given to tangents about cake, pigeons, buses and his late Aunt Hermione;\n512\t  - **Dazza Keegan**: a retired coastal fast bowler, blunt, \"mate\";\n513\t  - in reviews only, **Professor Priya Statsworth**, the Owl-Eye analyst (\"Clipping the bails by a whisker of a whisker\").\n514\t- **The director:**\n515\t  - It subscribes to the bus, scores candidate lines by event priority, and plays at most one exchange per ball (1–2 lines, the second often the other commentator's reply).\n516\t  - It respects `commentaryRate` *(T)* and stays quiet during the run-up and release; it talks from contact to `between`.\n517\t  - Lines are picked from pools by tag, with a per-line cooldown (no repeat within 20 balls), from `RNG.cosmetic`.\n518\t  - Template variables: `{batter} {bowler} {fielder} {team} {opp} {ground} {runs} {need} {balls} {target} {kmh} {speed} {item} {score}`.\n519\t  - Idle filler (pigeons, cake from listeners, buses passing) plays at most once per over.\n520\t- **Presentation:** an `E.Dialog` instance with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.6`, `place: 'bottom'`, alpha 0.85, and the speaker's portrait (a live Humanoid close-up), name in their colour. Text is typed with the gibberish voice (§5.13.3), at most 2 lines of ~42 characters, readable from 10 feet. Setting: Commentary captions on/off; voices have their own volume.\n521\t- **Narrative voice everywhere:**\n522\t  - Results show a 2–3-sentence **match report in Wisden style** (§8.9.3).\n523\t  - Loading lines (\"Rolling the pitch\", \"Whitening the creases\", \"Brewing the tea\", \"Polishing the ball on a trouser leg\", \"Counting the crowd's umbrellas\").\n524\t  - Menu tooltips, chapter cards and the Almanack-style ending all use the same dry, affectionate register.\n525\t- **Tests:** every event type has ≥ 3 lines per relevant commentator; no line exceeds 2 × 42 characters after template fill with the longest names; no repeat within 20 balls in a 200-ball bot run; captions are off when the setting is off.\n526\t\n527\t### 5.13 Audio (`40-audio.js`, `41-voice.js`, `18-data-music.js`)\n528\t\n529\t#### 5.13.1 SFX\n530\tEngine voices via `audio.define(name, voice)`, combining layers. Starting recipes:\n531\t\n532\t| Name | Recipe |\n533\t|---|---|\n534\t| `bat` (\"tock\") | `[{wave:'noise',freq:2400,to:900,dur:.04,vol:.35,filter:'bandpass'},{wave:'sine',freq:620,to:380,dur:.09,vol:.4},{wave:'triangle',freq:1240,dur:.05,vol:.15}]` |\n535\t| `batSweet` | the above + `{wave:'sine',freq:880,to:1320,dur:.12,vol:.2}` (a ring for PERFECT) |\n536\t| `edge` | `{wave:'noise',freq:5200,dur:.02,vol:.25,filter:'highpass'}` + a thin click |\n537\t| `pitchThud` | `{wave:'sine',freq:140,to:80,dur:.06,vol:.3}` + soft noise |\n538\t| `stumps` | wooden clatter: three short triangles at 700/540/860 Hz staggered 25 ms + noise; `bails`: two high ticks |\n539\t| `gloves` | keeper's pop (low noise burst, lowpass) |\n540\t| `throwWhoosh` | swept noise |\n541\t| `dive` | a long lowpass noise slide |\n542\t| `board` | ball hitting the boundary boards (wood knock) |\n543\t| `glass` | smash: a noise burst plus random high sine tinkles from RNG.cosmetic |\n544\t| `splash` | a noise burst falling from 3000 to 400 Hz (lowpass, 0.35 s) plus a low sine plop at 90 Hz, then a few soft droplet ticks |\n545\t| `gull` | pitch-swept squawk with vibrato |\n546\t| `moo` | a saw at 110→95 Hz, lowpass, vibrato |\n547\t| `bees` | a buzzing saw with tremolo |\n548\t| `steam` | the Thrumptonator: noise hiss plus a piston thump |\n549\t| `firework` | rising whistle plus a boom |\n550\t| `hypeFull`, `fireOn`, `special` | arpeggio stings |\n551\t| menu | `select`, `confirm`, `cancel` (engine presets) |\n552\t\n553\tThrottle with the engine's 35 ms duplicate guard.\n554\t\n555\t#### 5.13.2 The crowd engine\n556\tIts own Web Audio graph on `audio.bus('sfx')`:\n557\t- an **ambient bed**: pink-ish noise through 3 bandpass filters (200, 800 and 2000 Hz), level = ground crowd size × 0.15, with a slow LFO murmur;\n558\t- **applause**: dense random short noise grains (handclaps) at 30–90 per second, from RNG.cosmetic;\n559\t- **cheer and roar**: a vowel-formant \"AAAH\" made from 6 detuned saws through formant filters (F1 750, F2 1250);\n560\t- **\"OOOH\"** (F1 400, F2 800) for near misses, and a groan;\n561\t- **chant**: \"KER-FUF-FLE\" as three formant syllables on the music beat (`audio.onBeat`), with claps.\n562\t\n563\tIntensity follows events (a six gives a roar of 2 s and 30 s of excited murmur). Rain mutes it to 30% plus umbrella rustle.\n564\t\n565\t#### 5.13.3 Gibberish voices (Animalese/Simlish style)\n566\t`voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` turns text into syllables (vowel groups, with consonant onsets):\n567\t- **Each syllable** is one source (saw for voiced sounds, plus a short noise burst for plosive and fricative onsets). It goes through two bandpass formant filters set to the syllable's vowel (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 300/2300, o 500/900, u 350/700 Hz; Q ≈ 6) and a gain envelope (attack 8 ms, hold, release 30 ms). Length is 55–110 ms × profile speed.\n568\t- **Pitch** = profile base × (1 + small RNG.cosmetic jitter ±4%), × a declination of −12% across the phrase. It rises 25% at '?', with +30% and louder for '!' and CAPS. Gaps of 40 ms at spaces, 150 ms at punctuation.\n569\t- **Profiles** (a blank speed means 1.0):\n570\t\n571\t  | Speaker | Base | Speed | Character |\n572\t  |---|---|---|---|\n573\t  | Aubrey | 105 Hz | 1.25 (slow) | vibrato 5 Hz |\n574\t  | Dazza | 135 Hz | 0.85 | flat |\n575\t  | Priya | 210 Hz | 0.9 | crisp |\n576\t  | Edie | 240 Hz | 0.9 | |\n577\t  | Basil | 120 Hz | | tremolo, wobbly |\n578\t  | Dev | 170 Hz | | precise |\n579\t  | Pip | 290 Hz | fast | |\n580\t  | Mrs Crumb | 220 Hz | | warm, low Q |\n581\t  | Rev. Hollis | 150 Hz | | soft |\n582\t  | Thrumpton | 160 Hz | | nasal: formants ×1.2, glide |\n583\t  | Ghosts | | | noise-voiced whisper, reverb (feedback delay 180 ms) |\n584\t  | Thrumptonator | 90 Hz | | ring-modulated at 30 Hz |\n585\t  | Players | | | random base 140–260 Hz |\n586\t\n587\t- **Uses:** the Dialog's typing calls `onSyllable` to sync the portrait's mouth. Short shouts (\"YES!\", \"NO!\", \"WAIT!\", \"HOWZAT!\", \"OUT!\") use the same synth with exaggerated pitch envelopes.\n588\t- Route through `audio.bus('voice')` (engine change 3). Create the nodes lazily after the first gesture (the engine's `audio.ctx`).\n589\t\n590\t#### 5.13.4 Music\n591\tOriginal songs in the engine's step notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes, name, on }] }`), each with named layers for dynamic intensity: `base`, `drums` (in when the Hype meter is > 50), `fire` (in when ON FIRE). Matches play the ground's track at low volume under the crowd. Between overs the music rises; during deliveries it ducks (`audio.layer`). Stings use `audio.sting(song, { at: 'beat' })`.\n592\t\n593\t| Track | Feel | Notes for the composer |\n594\t|---|---|---|\n595\t| `title` | \"The Kerfuffle March\": jaunty brass-band march, 120 bpm, square lead and triangle tuba bass, snare rolls | I–IV–V in B♭, a cheeky chromatic run every 4th bar |\n596\t| `map` | the minibus travels: bouncy 6/8 | |\n597\t| `green` | pastoral summer afternoon, 96 bpm, pulse-12 woodwind lead | cuckoo-like two-note motif |\n598\t| `hall` | stately minuet with a harpsichord-like square arpeggio, 3/4 | |\n599\t| `bay` | surf-rock: saw with vibrato as twangy guitar, driving drums, 150 bpm | |\n600\t| `downs` | the conjurer's mystery waltz: minor key, tremolo | storm variant with rain drums |\n601\t| `dome` | stadium rock anthem: chant-able hook, 128 bpm, layered | a spooky organ layer for the ghosts |\n602\t| `tea` | gentle waltz for tea intervals and story scenes | |\n603\t| `showdown` | a tense countdown loop | |\n604\t| `attract` | a title remix | |\n605\t\n606\tStings: `four`, `six`, `wicket`, `over`, `hype`, `fire`, `special`, `win` (fanfare), `lose` (sad trombone slide), `toss`, `chapter`.\n607\t\n608\t#### 5.13.5 Tests\n609\tHeadless audio is silent, so test the structure:\n610\t- every `sfx()` name used in the code is defined;\n611\t- `voice.syllables('Howzat!')` gives 2 syllables with a rising last pitch;\n612\t- songs parse (each track's token count equals `steps × bars` for its length) and stay in their note range;\n613\t- music layers switch on `fire:on`;\n614\t- the `end` hub event mutes everything.\n615\t\n616\tIn a browser smoke run, `audio.failed` is null after a synthetic key press.\n617\t\n618\t### 5.14 Input and controls\n619\t\n620\t#### 5.14.1 The action map\n621\tBuilt from `E.Input.DEFAULT` with the engine's attack/dash/skill/jump removed, these actions added, and menu confirm/cancel overridden so J selects and L backs out, matching gameplay.\n622\t\n623\t| Action | Batting | Bowling | Fielding | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n624\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n625\t| move/aim | shot direction | pitch marker; aftertouch | steer the active fielder | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | pointer (aim and marker) |\n626\t| `shot` | ground shot | delivery 1, meter release | catch, dive, throw to striker's end | ✕ (Pad0) | J | left click |\n627\t| `loft` | lofted shot | delivery 2 | throw to bowler's end | □ (Pad2) | K | right click |\n628\t| `block` | defend | delivery 3 | lob back to keeper | ○ (Pad1) | L, Q | — |\n629\t| `special` | signature shot; REVIEW | signature delivery; REVIEW | — | △ (Pad3) | I, F | — |\n630\t| `run` | run / again / dive | cycle field preset | switch fielder | R1 (Pad5) | Space | — |\n631\t| `back` | stay / send back | over/round the wicket | — | L1 (Pad4) | Shift | — |\n632\t| `charge` | charge down the pitch | — | — | R2 (Pad7) | E | — |\n633\t| `pause` | engine | | | Options (Pad9) | Esc, P | corner button |\n634\t| `confirm` / `cancel` (menus) | | | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space, J / Esc, Backspace, L | click / — |\n635\t\n636\t- Mouse clicks also pick menu items and things in the world: stumps to throw at, fielders in the field-preset preview, pins on the story map, and the review button.\n637\t- **Hover moves the focus** in every menu (E.Menu does this; our widgets in `45-ui.js` must too).\n638\t- The Backquote key (`) toggles the dev panel. It's keyboard-only and not rebindable.\n639\t- Create (Pad8) is never bound (in hub sessions it belongs to the hub).\n640\t- All rebindable through the engine's Controls screen. Pass `menu.controls` names: `{ shot: 'Ground shot / Bowl / Catch', loft: 'Lofted shot / Alt delivery', block: 'Defend / Alt delivery', special: 'Special / Review', run: 'Run / Field', back: 'Stay / Over-Round', charge: 'Charge' }`.\n641\t- **Two players on one keyboard:** build each half's preset with `E.Input.halfKeys(KERFUFFLE_KEYS, 'left' | 'right')`. List the actions in the order shot, loft, block, run, back, special, so they land on F G H R T Y (left half) and on `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` (right half). halfKeys has six buttons, so add **charge** by hand: Q for the left half, Numpad0 for the right. Check neither collides, and that the mouse stays with player 1. The Controls screen shows a page per player.\n642\t\n643\t#### 5.14.2 Mouse feel\n644\tIn batting, the aim arrow follows the pointer's ground point (`game.mouseGround()`). Left and right clicks are the two main shots, like the classic mouse-click browser cricket games PC players know. In bowling, the marker sits under the pointer, a click starts the run-up, and a click releases. In fielding, the active fielder runs toward the pointer while the right button is held (optional) and a click on stumps throws. The cursor is a small cricket-ball reticle drawn by the game; the system cursor is hidden over the canvas only in play.\n645\t\n646\t#### 5.14.3 Glyphs and prompts (`43-glyphs.js`)\n647\t- `prompt(action)` draws an **icon** for the binding on `input.lastDevice`:\n648\t  - **gamepad:** PlayStation glyphs by default. ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fd38a` shapes on dark discs, 9×9 px. Shoulder and trigger pills read L1 R1 L2 R2, and there are a stick and a d-pad icon. With the **Button symbols** setting on **Xbox**: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs, and LB RB LT RT pills. Read the symbol family with `input.padStyle()`, set by the engine's `glyphs` setting (engine change 4).\n649\t  - **keyboard:** a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` ('J', 'Space', 'Shift').\n650\t  - **mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit. Actions without a mouse binding fall back to their keycap.\n651\t- Find the code from the action's bindings (`input.map[action]` after the player's rebinding) filtered by device: gamepad codes `Pad\\d+`, mouse `Mouse\\d`, else keys. Don't render `input.label()`'s strings ✕ ○ □ △ through `E.font`: the 5×7 font lacks those glyphs.\n652\t- Prompts change on the very next frame after the device changes. The helper `prompts([['shot','GROUND'],['loft','LOFT']])` lays out a row.\n653\t- **Tests:** after `press('KeyJ')` the shot prompt shows the keycap J; after `press('Pad0')` it's a PS cross; with the setting on xbox it's an A disc; after a mouse click the LMB icon; rebinding shot to KeyU shows U.\n654\t\n655\t### 5.15 UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n656\t\n657\t#### 5.15.1 Screen and type\n658\t- `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (320×240 at least; a 16:9 TV shows 427×240), so every HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n659\t- Body text uses the 5×7 font at scale 1 with a drop shadow (31 px tall on a 1080p TV). Headings use scale 2. Logos use `E.font.title`.\n660\t- One decision per screen. At most 6 menu items. No tables of numbers, no scrolling lists of more than 8 rows, no paragraphs of more than 3 lines.\n661\t- The **focus** is a big animated cricket-ball cursor plus a gold highlight bar. Every focusable element has a hover rectangle for the mouse.\n662\t- Footer prompts on every screen: \"✕ Select ○ Back\", using live glyphs.\n663\t- Keep the engine's top-right corner area clear (`game.cornerArea()`).\n664\t\n665\t#### 5.15.2 The match HUD (pixel overlay, `44-hud.js`)\n666\t- **Score bug** (top left): the batting team's 3-letter code with its colour chip, a big `47/2`, and overs `3.4`. When chasing, a second line: \"NEED 12 FROM 8\" (gold if gettable, red if hard).\n667\t- **This over** strip (top right): 6 balls as small discs (`•` dot, 1 2 3, 4 blue, 6 gold, W red, wd/nb grey).\n668\t- **Striker card** (bottom left): a mini portrait plus \"PLUCKROSE 31* (14)\". **Bowler card** (bottom right): \"TWISTLETON 1-12\".\n669\t- **Kerfuffle meter** (bottom centre): 120×6 bar, with \"△ SPECIAL\" when full and a flame overlay when ON FIRE.\n670\t- **Transient callouts** (centre, big, 0.9 s): FOUR!, SIX! MAXIMUM!, WICKET!, FREE HIT, ON FIRE!, KERFUFFLE BONUS +10, PERFECT.\n671\t- **Contextual prompts** sit near the action: \"R1 RUN / L1 STAY\" by the striker, the catch ring and \"✕ CATCH!\" at the ball, the throw picker icons at both stumps. Tutorial hints (chapter 1, Nets) show the full control row.\n672\t- In bowling: the delivery panel (4 glyph buttons with names), the meter (140×10, bottom centre) and the speed readout after release.\n673\t- In wide cam only: a **radar** (top right, under the over strip): an ellipse with fielders as team-colour dots and the ball as a white dot.\n674\t- The commentary caption sits at the bottom centre, shown from contact onward, never during the run-up.\n675\t- **Tests:** the HUD draws on 320×240, 427×240, 640×270 and 320×690 (via `--size` in the browser smoke) without overlapping the corner area or each other. Write a simple rectangle-overlap check in the HUD layout code, unit-tested.\n676\t\n677\t#### 5.15.3 Screens\n678\t| Screen | Contents |\n679\t|---|---|\n680\t| Title | the village green at golden hour, the pavilion, a batter knocking up, Doris the cow, the logo, and \"PRESS\" with the confirm glyph for the last device. Then a menu of six: STORY (opens Continue, when a save exists, and New Game), QUICK MATCH, VERSUS, SUPER OVER SHOWDOWN, NETS, EXTRAS (opens Settings and About/Credits). |\n681\t| Story map | a painted county map, 5 ground pins on a road `E.Path`, the minibus driving between them, 3 stars per chapter, Play / Replay |\n682\t| Cutscene | a three-quarter vignette (the pavilion tea room, Thrumpton's car on the green) with posed rigs, `E.Dialog` with portraits and markup (`{shake}`, `{wave}`, `{c=#..}`), skippable with a hold-✕ ring (1 s) |\n683\t| Match setup | big rows: Teams (left/right cycles a team card with kit and stars), Ground (a card with a panorama thumbnail), Overs 1–5, Wickets 2/3/5/10, Difficulty, Time of day, Weather, Kerfuffle rules, Players (join status). One row focused at a time, values change with left/right or a mouse click on the arrows. |\n684\t| Toss | captains on the square, a coin flipped in 3D (sprite rotation frames), \"Call: ✕ HEADS ○ TAILS\", then \"BAT ✕ / BOWL ○\" |\n685\t| Bowler select | 3–5 big portrait cards (type icon, overs left, special name) |\n686\t| Innings break / Tea interval | \"TEA\" card, a summary in 3 big numbers (runs, wickets, top scorer), the target. In story, pick a tea treat (§8.7.1, 3 cards). |\n687\t| Results | winner banner, margin, Player of the Match portrait, 3 key stats, the Wisden-style report (≤ 3 sentences), stars earned (story), \"▶ Highlights\" (the highlight reel), Rematch / Continue / Menu |\n688\t| About / Credits | paged (◀ ▶): 1 Source Information, 2 Tech and Engine, 3 Art, Animation and Audio, 4 Online and Testing, 5 Testing Feedback, 6 Engine Feedback, 7 Prompt Feedback, 8 Credits. One topic per page, ≤ 10 short lines. |\n689\t| Pause (engine) | Resume, our items (Restart Match, Match Info, Dev Mode), then the engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title. In hub sessions there is no Restart, Dev Mode or Quit (`menu.quit: false`); Full screen stays (the client hands it to the hub). |\n690\t| Showdown | choose Bat or Bowl, play the over, then initials and a table (`E.Arcade.enter` / `drawTable`) |\n691\t| Nets | lessons list, free practice (choose deliveries for the bowling machine or a dummy batter), Six Machine (12 balls, targets, score) |\n692\t\n693\t#### 5.15.4 Settings (`menu.settings`, group 'Game'; plus the engine's standard groups)\n694\t\n695\t| Setting | Values (default first) |\n696\t|---|---|\n697\t| Difficulty | County / Village / Test / Kerfuffle! |\n698\t| Default overs | 2 / 1 / 3 / 5 |\n699\t| Default wickets | 5 / 3 / 10 / 2 |\n700\t| Kerfuffle rules | On / Off |\n701\t| Pitch marker (batting) | On / Off |\n702\t| Timing feedback | On / Off |\n703\t| Fielding | Assisted / Manual / Auto |\n704\t| Running | Manual / Auto |\n705\t| Replays | Big moments / Always / Never |\n706\t| Commentary captions | On / Off |\n707\t| Commentary voices | On / Off |\n708\t\n709\tButton symbols live in the engine's Controller group (PlayStation by default, or Xbox). Voices volume lives in Sound (engine change 3). The Comfort group (reduce motion, shake, flashes) is the engine's, and it is honoured by all our FX.\n710\t\n711\t#### 5.15.5 Assists\n712\tFielding: Assisted catches easy chances automatically, and Auto fields everything. Running: Auto follows the AI's YES and NO. Pitch marker. Difficulty. These go in Settings and in match setup.\n713\t\n714\t#### 5.15.6 Touch-only devices\n715\t- **Detection:** `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, and no gamepad or keyboard event seen yet.\n716\t- The game creates **no touch buttons** (no scene declares `touch`; set the engine's touch-button setting to hidden).\n717\t- The title shows a large panel: **\"Controller or keyboard required\"**, a pad and keyboard pictogram, and a big **Settings** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`; the engine's corner menu button stays too). No other menu entries respond to taps.\n718\t- **The attract mode still runs** after 10 s idle. A tap exits it back to this panel.\n719\t- The moment a gamepad connects or a key is pressed, the panel goes and the normal title appears.\n720\t- **Test:** headless with a stubbed `matchMedia` gives `snapshot().ui.touchGate === true`, no touch buttons, and attract starting after 10 s. A `press('Pad0')` clears the gate.\n721\t\n722\t### 5.16 Modes\n723\t\n724\t#### 5.16.1 Story: *The Grand Kerfuffle*\n725\t5 chapters (§8.8) on the story map. Each chapter is a `MatchConfig` scenario plus scripted beats: `game.script` generators that wait on bus events, for example \"after innings 1, play the scene `thrumptonArrives`\". Each has 3 **Kerfuffle Goals** (stars), and a loss offers Retry or \"Retry on Village difficulty\".\n726\t- Chapter 1 teaches through **lessons in the Nets** (§5.16.3), then plays the match with contextual tips.\n727\t- Saves go through `game.saves`' autosave slot (§5.20), with `saves.write = () => ({ data: profile.story, place: chapterName, level: n })`. Don't set `menu: { saves: true }`: the story has no save points.\n728\t- Quick Match unlocks a team when you beat it. Dev mode can unlock all.\n729\t\n730\t#### 5.16.2 Quick Match\n731\tAny 2 of the 6 teams, any of the 5 grounds, plus the setup options. 1–4 players can join here (§5.16.5).\n732\t\n733\t#### 5.16.3 Nets\n734\t- **Lessons** (scripted, each checks success before moving on):\n735\t  1. Aim and ground shot.\n736\t  2. Lofted shot.\n737\t  3. Defend and leave.\n738\t  4. Running (with a fielder).\n739\t  5. Charge vs spin.\n740\t  6. Bowling: marker and meter.\n741\t  7. Aftertouch.\n742\t  8. Specials and Hype.\n743\t  9. Catch ring.\n744\t  10. Throws.\n745\t  11. Reviews.\n746\t\n747\t  Chapter 1 runs lessons 1–4 and 6. Later chapters run the others when first needed, from the chapter's beats.\n748\t- **Free practice:** the bowling machine with chosen deliveries, line and length (or random), or bowling at a dummy batter.\n749\t- **Six Machine:** 12 balls; score = runs + target bonuses; with a table.\n750\t\n751\t#### 5.16.4 Super Over Showdown\n752\t- **Bat:** 6 balls from the Thrumptonator 3000 (seeded deliveries, a mixed set: express, bouncer, yorker, slower ball, banana swing, Triple Threat as the 6th ball), 2 wickets, Kerfuffle rules on. Score = runs.\n753\t- **Bowl:** defend 14 against Kev Malone (a slogger), and score = (14 − runs conceded) × 10 + wickets × 25.\n754\t- `E.Arcade` tables per variant with initials. The same Bat mode is the AI Hub round (§5.17).\n755\t\n756\t#### 5.16.5 Versus and local multiplayer (1–4 on one screen)\n757\t- In match setup, players join through `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`: Start on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (which gives the keyboard's right half). Each joined player moves left or right to pick a team (player colours `E.PLAYER_COLORS`).\n758\t- **Roles:**\n759\t  - 1v1: each human does everything for their side.\n760\t  - 2v2, or 2 humans co-op vs CPU: when batting, the two humans are the two batters. Each controls running for their own man, and the one on strike also plays the shots, so mix-ups are possible (§5.4). When bowling, they alternate overs, and the other takes the fielding (catch rings and throws).\n761\t  - 2v1: the solo player does all roles for their side.\n762\t- One shared camera, because cricket is a one-camera sport. Prompts appear by each player's actor in their colour, using their own device's glyphs (`players[i].lastDevice`).\n763\t- The pause shows who paused (\"P2 PAUSED\", engine).\n764\t- **Tests:** `game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })` gives P2 the bowler in a 1v1; co-op running agreement; mix-up run-out; the per-player prompt device.\n765\t\n766\t### 5.17 Online: AI Hub session (parallel Super Over) (`60-hub.js`)\n767\t\n768\t#### 5.17.1 Tags (in `<head>`, after the client)\n769\t```html\n770\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n771\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n772\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"parallel\">\n773\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"2\">\n774\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Most runs from one over (6 balls, 2 wickets) wins\">\n775\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Hit: Cross/J/click · Loft: Square/K · Run: R1/Space\">\n776\t```\n777\tKeep the goal ≤ 60 characters and the howto ≤ 80 characters; the bundle test counts them.\n778\t\n779\t#### 5.17.2 Flow when `AIHub.active`\n780\t1. Skip the title, attract and menus. Boot runs as normal. `AIHub.active` is already true on the first line, so decide at `new E.Game(...)`: pass `autoPause: false` (frames in the harness don't all have focus, and a paused copy would stall its round) and `menu: { quit: false, ... }`, and set `game.hubActive = true` (engine change 5).\n781\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, start the normal standalone game.\n782\t3. Build the round from `info.seed` via `AIHub.rng('over')`: the 6 deliveries (type, target, kmh), the ground (always the Megadome at night, so it looks the same for all), the batting side (Kerfuffle CC: Edie and Dev open, Pip comes in at the first wicket), and the field (the defensive preset).\n783\t4. **Our card** (scene `hubcard`): the Megadome at night, the Thrumptonator steaming, and the players in their seat colours (`info.players`, names drawn as pixel text, never inserted as HTML) with READY ticks from the `card` event. Below: \"MOST RUNS FROM ONE OVER WINS\", this player's controls as glyph prompts for their last device, and the time left (`card.left`), plus \"Paused: most players want more time\" and \"Starting…\" states. ✕/Enter/click on READY calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △/T/click on MORE TIME calls `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card draws no `Math.random`: cosmetic noise comes from `AIHub.rng('card')`. It changes nothing in the round.\n784\t5. Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the round is built, with no key press needed.\n785\t6. On `start`: a countdown \"3, 2, 1, PLAY!\" while `AIHub.time() < 0`. The first ball's run-up begins at `time ≥ 0`.\n786\t7. Play the over (scene `hubround`, the match machinery with `mode: 'hub'`). The pause menu has no Quit, Restart or Dev Mode (§5.15.3), and there are no replays (the clock is ticking).\n787\t8. Call `AIHub.progress({ score: runs, label: runs + '/' + wkts + ' (' + balls + ' balls)' })` at the start and after every ball.\n788\t9. The over ends after 6 legal balls or 2 wickets: call `AIHub.result({ score: runs, stats: { 'boundaries': b, 'sixes': s, 'best shot': name } })` once. Then show \"Waiting for the others…\" over the Megadome crowd.\n789\t10. On `time-up`, if no result has been sent: call `result({ score: runs })` at once.\n790\t11. On `end`: stop input (`game.input.clear()` plus a flag that ignores all actions), stop music and sfx (`audio.stopMusic()`, mute), and freeze the scene with \"Round over\", or \"You sat this one out\" for reason `sat-out`. The hub shows the results.\n791\t12. Never call `send()`. No storage carries over (the client gives empty storage). No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`.\n792\t\n793\t#### 5.17.3 Tests\n794\t- **hub-mock suite (Node):** a fake `AIHub` object before the game loads checks that `connect` is called with parallel and [1, 4], `ready` is called with no input, the deliveries are identical across two runs with the same seed and different player inputs, there's one progress per ball, one result, a time-up mid-over gives an immediate result, and `end` stops input and audio.\n795\t- **browser-hub suite:**\n796\t  1. Copy the bundle to `test/hub/game.html` beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js`.\n797\t  2. Serve with `tools/serve.mjs` and confirm the title tag.\n798\t  3. Run Playwright at `harness.html?game=game.html&players=1&auto=1`, then `players=2`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`.\n799\t  4. Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` (90 s) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Print any failing check names and details.\n800\t  5. Open the bundle outside a session (`file://`) and see the title scene.\n801\t\n802\t### 5.18 Attract mode (`51-attract.js`)\n803\t- **Trigger:** on the title and every front-end menu scene (main menu, mode select, setup, story map), the `idle` timer resets on any input: an action press, any key, mouse movement (`input.mouse.t` changed), a click, any gamepad button or stick past the dead zone (`input.padMove`), or a touch. At **10 s** it calls `game.go('attract', { segment: next })`. Never in hub sessions.\n804\t- **Exit:** any input (the same list) returns at once to the title and consumes the input (`input.consumeAll()`, so it doesn't also pick a menu item). The idle timer restarts. Nothing is saved; settings are untouched.\n805\t- **What it shows:** a looping **showreel** of real gameplay on the real match machinery, with cheating autopilots on both sides (perfect timing, chosen outcomes, guaranteed catches, never-ending innings). It cycles through 10 segments (~25 s each), each a small data script `{ ground, timeOfDay, weather, teams, setup, balls: [{ delivery, target, shot, dir, grade, outcome, extra }] }`:\n806\t  1. Village Green: Doris the cow ambles across the outfield; a cover drive runs through a Golden Teapot token (item collected) for FOUR (wide cam, applause, commentary).\n807\t  2. A lofted straight SIX through the pub window (target smash, slow-mo, replay).\n808\t  3. Basil's leg-break hits the pad: a huge appeal, given NOT OUT, the Kerfuffle Review System, Owl-Eye, OUT. The next batter is bowled by the googly (stumps cartwheel, the umpire's OUT signal).\n809\t  4. Thrumpton Hall, overcast: an outswinger edged to slip, then a diving catch on a PERFECT ring.\n810\t  5. Sunburnt Bay: a bouncer ducked, then the next one hooked for six into the sea, and the tide event.\n811\t  6. A quick single, then a direct-hit run-out, third umpire, OUT.\n812\t  7. Dustbowl Downs: the Vanishing Ball special, a stumping, then the rain delay with the covers and the Kerfuffle Method card.\n813\t  8. Three boundaries, ON FIRE, the Tea-Kettle Slog special onto the jumbotron.\n814\t  9. The Megadome at night in fog: the Thrumptonator's Triple Threat, ghost fielders, fireworks, a victory celebration and a Wisden-style report card.\n815\t  10. The high-score table (`cab.drawTable`) and a \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" title flash.\n816\t\n817\t  Then it loops.\n818\t- **On screen:** \"DEMO\" blinking (bottom centre) and \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the device-appropriate glyph), plus a TV-style caption per segment (the ground's name). Segments start with a wipe transition.\n819\t- **Tests:**\n820\t  - **attract-coverage suite:** idle 10 s on the title gives the attract scene. Advance 12 minutes of game time: every segment ran at least once and the coverage flags were set (item, chaos, four, six, target, review, bowled, caught, run-out, third umpire, stumping, special, ON FIRE, rain, fog, ghosts, night), with no errors.\n821\t  - Then `press('KeyA')` returns to the title, a mouse move (`input.feed('move', ...)`) exits, and `press('Pad3')` exits.\n822\t  - Touch-only: attract runs and a tap exits.\n823\t\n824\t### 5.19 Dev mode (`70-dev.js`)\n825\t- **Reach:**\n826\t  - the **pause menu item \"Dev Mode\"** (`menu.items`) in every pausable scene;\n827\t  - Backquote as a keyboard shortcut;\n828\t  - hidden in hub sessions.\n829\t\n830\t  It opens as a full-screen 10-foot panel over the paused game. Values apply instantly. Resume to see them in play.\n831\t- **Navigation:** L1 and R1 (or Tab, or a click on a tab) switch tabs. Up and down (or hover) select a row. Left and right adjust; hold to accelerate; the mouse drags slider knobs and the wheel scrolls. ✕ toggles or activates, △ resets the selected row to its default, ○ goes back.\n832\t  - **\"Reset all to defaults\"** is the first item of the TUNING tab.\n833\t  - A **\"DEV\" badge** shows on the HUD while any value differs from its default.\n834\t- **Persistence:** tuning persists through `E.store.set('kerfuffle:dev', ...)` (dev mode is hidden in hub sessions, whose storage is memory-only anyway).\n835\t\n836\t#### 5.19.1 Tabs\n837\t1. **TUNING** (sliders, each showing its current value against the default):\n838\t\n839\t   | Key | Label | Default | Min | Max | Step |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"439664c9-8f3d-44b7-877b-cdcf21baedf8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:28:13.985Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062757__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 5.8 AI (`27-ai.js`)\n- **Difficulty** multiplies TUNING (§5.19.2): Village, County, Test, Kerfuffle!.\n- **CPU batting:**\n  1. Read the ball: the predictor, plus a perception error σ = `aiBatSkill` *(T)* 55 ms × (1.3 vs mystery or special balls, 0.8 vs full tosses) × batter technique.\n  2. Choose the intent from the match situation: required rate vs current rate, wickets in hand, balls left, plus `aiAggression` *(T)* 0.5 and the batter's style (slogger, accumulator, blocker). Ball type: full and straight → drive or loft; short → pull or hook (or duck if hook-shy); wide outside off → cut or leave; good length on the stumps → defend unless aggressive; yorker → block or helicopter if a slogger.\n  3. Choose a direction: the largest angular gap between fielders within 25 units for ground shots; for lofted shots, the direction with the fewest deep fielders, toward the shorter boundary.\n  4. Press at the predicted time + the error. Run calls as in §5.4.\n- **CPU bowling:** a per-batter plan from the bowler's type, weighted by the batter's weaknesses (data: `weak: ['short','spin']`). Pace: mostly good length on off stump, a bouncer every ~5, a yorker at the death. Swing: an outswinger set-up then an inswinger. Spin: flighted outside off, then a googly or doosra. The target scatter is `aiBowlAccuracy` *(T)* 10 units × the meter result (offsets drawn by skill). Use the special when Hype is full in the last over or vs a set batter.\n- **CPU captain:** picks the next bowler (one not over the limit, preferring a type change and a bowler who suits the ground), picks a field preset by situation (attacking when defending a big total or vs a new batter; defensive at the death), and chooses bat or bowl at the toss (bat on a flat pitch, bowl when overcast).\n- **CPU fielding:** §5.5.\n- **Autopilot** (`ai.autopilot(side, role, { cheat })`) produces **virtual input** through `input.hold/tap` exactly as a player's would. It powers the bots in tests, the dev toggles Auto-bat and Auto-bowl, and the attract mode. `cheat` options: perfect timing, a chosen shot or outcome, a guaranteed catch, never out.\n- **Tests:** the bot-match suite (CPU vs CPU, each ground, plausible statistics, §11.3); the AI never presses after the contact plane; an aggressive chaser with 20 needed off 6 lofts more than 50% of balls; a defensive AI at 0 required leaves or blocks.\n\n### 5.9 Hype, ON FIRE and specials (`28-hype.js`)\n\n**The Kerfuffle meter (Hype)** belongs to each team, 0–100, and is shown at the bottom centre. Gains are × `hypeGain` *(T)*.\n\n| Gain | Batting | Bowling or fielding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Large | six +25, stadium target +30 | wicket +30, maiden +20 |\n| Medium | four +15, three runs +10 | screamer catch +15, direct hit +15 |\n| Small | PERFECT +8 | beaten the bat +8, dot ball +6 |\n| Any side | chaos crowd moments +5 | |\n\nAt 100, △ \"SPECIAL READY\" pulses and the crowd chants \"KER-FUF-FLE!\". Using the special empties the meter.\n- **Specials** belong to characters (§8.6): the batting special uses the current striker's, the bowling special the current bowler's. A player without one uses the generic *Kerfuffle Shot* (a guaranteed PERFECT lofted hit, fire trail) or *Kerfuffle Ball* (a green-meter delivery with +15% pace and double swing).\n- **ON FIRE:**\n  - **Batter:** `fireStreak` *(T)* 3 consecutive boundaries. The bat burns with flame particles and the music brings in its `fire` layer (`audio.layer('fire', 1)`, §5.13.4). Timing windows ×1.5 and shot power ×1.2. It ends on a dot ball or a wicket, or after 6 balls.\n  - **Bowler:** 3 consecutive dot balls or wickets. The ball burns with a trail: pace +10%, edge zone ×1.4. It ends when a boundary is conceded, or after 6 balls.\n  - Commentary and crowd go wild, with a sting and a screen flash (respecting the Comfort settings).\n- **Tests:** three boundaries give `fire:on` and widened windows; a dot ball gives `fire:off`; at Hype 100, △ consumes it and emits `special`; each special's effect flag is observable in the snapshot (e.g. `ball.fx === 'vanish'` during its invisible window).\n\n### 5.10 Items, stadium targets, chaos events, weather and time of day (`29-chaos.js`)\n- **Kerfuffle rules** (a match setting, on in story from chapter 2, default on in Quick Match) enable items and specials. Off gives \"purist arcade\": no items, no specials, no chaos (so players can see what is core and what is extra).\n- **Items** (§8.7.1):\n  - At the start of each over, with probability 0.35 × `chaosRate` *(T)* per ground, a batting item token floats over a random outfield spot (an RNG.play seed). The ball passing through its 14-unit sphere (in the air or along the ground) gives it to the batting side.\n  - Bowling items are earned by 2 dot balls in an over.\n  - Items **auto-apply** on their next relevant event. No extra buttons: the HUD shows a held item icon labelled \"ARMED\".\n- **Stadium targets** (§8.3): each ground has 3–4 landmark boxes in world space (a sphere or box per target). A ball whose flight passes through one triggers it: bonus runs added to the batting score as \"KERFUFFLE BONUS +N\", Hype +30, slow-mo 0.4 s, particles, crowd and commentary, plus a target state (a broken window sprite, a spinning weathervane, a jumbotron glitch). It resets each match. Bonus runs count toward totals and targets, and the scorecard lists them as \"Bonus\".\n- **Chaos events** (§8.7.3) are scripted mini-events with a per-ground pool, firing between balls (never mid-delivery) at a per-over chance × `chaosRate` *(T)*. Examples: Doris the cow wanders onto the outfield and blocks a fielding lane for 2 overs (fielders path round her); seagulls steal a ball stopped in the outfield (byes); bees chase a fielder (−30% speed for 1 over); the tide comes in; the rain delay; dust devils; ghost fielders phasing; Thrumpton's advert break.\n- **Weather and time of day:**\n  - `game.weather` for rain (the delay) and dust or leaves (cosmetic).\n  - **Fog** is our own screen overlay: dithered translucency (`px.blend`) whose strength grows with distance from the camera focus. Fielders beyond 400 units fade, and the ball is only visible within 260 units of the batter unless lit. It is drawn after the world and before the HUD.\n  - **Overcast:** swing ×1.5, ambient 0.78 with a blue-grey tint.\n  - **Day:** lights enabled with ambient 0.92, plus moving cloud-shadow decals.\n  - **Dusk:** ambient 0.7, warm.\n  - **Night:** ambient 0.42; four floodlight pylons each add `lights.add(x, y, 220, 1100, .75, { color: '#fff4dc' })`; the ball is drawn `emissive`.\n- **Tests:** a forced item spawn on the ball's predicted path is collected and applied; a target hit adds its bonus and emits `target:hit`; each chaos event can be forced (dev and test) and resolves within its time without errors; overcast doubles the measured swing displacement vs clear (allowing for the 1.5 factor); night enables 4 lights.\n\n### 5.11 Camera director and replays (`34-camera.js`, `35-replay.js`)\n\n#### 5.11.1 Views\nFour custom orthographic views, made once. Never tween zoom in a match: each new zoom re-bakes the TileMap floor, because `_floor` caches by view id, yaw, pitch and scale. So use fixed views and cut between them, like TV.\n\n| View | `new E.View(...)` | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| broadcast | `('broadcast','Broadcast', 0, T('camPitch') 12, T('camScale') 1.45, 1)` | every delivery: bowler at the bottom, pitch, batter, keeper and slips at the top, the far-end panorama behind them |\n| wide | `('wide','Wide', 0, 55, T('wideScale') 0.8, 1)` | ball in play round the ground |\n| review | `('review','Side-on', 90, 8, 2.4, 1)` | third umpire and run-out replays: the crease seen side-on |\n| close | `('close','Close-up', 0, 22, 2.6, 1)` | wickets, celebrations, umpire signals, specials' wind-ups |\n\n- Scenes set `camera: { zoom: false }` (the director frames shots; no player zoom). `game.setView(view)` cuts, and the engine snaps the camera on a view change.\n- **Why pitch 12° and scale 1.45:** a strongly foreshortened, telephoto TV look. A Humanoid about 29 units tall draws 29 × cos 12° × 1.45 ≈ 41 px. The 300 units from the bowler's crease to the striker's crease take 300 × sin 12° × 1.45 ≈ 90 px. On a 240-px-tall screen that places the striker's feet at about 47% of the height and the bowler's crease at about 85%. The keeper (y −160, 48 px above the striker's feet), the slips and the far-end panorama fill the top, and the bowler runs into view from below the bottom edge. In M1, check that the TileMap floor bakes correctly at this low pitch; if it doesn't, use 15° and scale 1.35.\n- **Warm the floor bakes** in a boot step (\"Mowing the outfield\"): draw one frame in each view with `game.advance(0, { draw: true })`, or by calling `map.drawFloor` on an offscreen renderer once per view. The first cut then never hitches.\n\n#### 5.11.2 The director\n- A small state machine of named shots: `broadcastSetup`, `broadcastBall`, `wideFollow`, `boundaryCrowd`, `wicketClose`, `catchFollow`, `reviewSideOn`, `celebration`, `overCard`.\n- **The broadcast framing:** choose the focus point each frame so the striker's feet sit at 47% of `r.H` and the bowler's popping crease at about 85%, on any aspect ratio. A taller screen shows more panorama above and more run-up below; a wider one shows more of the off and leg sides.\n- **Cut rules:**\n  - Cut to **wide** 0.12 s after contact when the predicted ball path leaves the broadcast frame within 0.8 s (the screen's edges, or north of y −185, behind the panorama, §6.3), or the shot is lofted.\n  - Stay **broadcast** for blocks, leaves, misses, edges to the keeper and slips, and stumpings.\n  - In **wide**, `game.focus` leads the ball: the point between the ball and its predicted landing or interception point, with `cam.smooth = 0.22`. At the boundary, hold on the crowd for 0.8 s.\n  - For a **wicket**, cut to close on the stumps or catcher at timeScale 0.35 for 0.8 s, then to the umpire's signal.\n  - A **review** runs on the review cam (§5.11.4).\n  - **Between balls**, hard-cut back to broadcast. Every camera change inside a match is a hard cut; the engine's fades (`game.go(..., { fade })`) are only for scene changes.\n- **Screen shake and flash** respect the Comfort settings (`game.shake`, `game.flash`).\n\n#### 5.11.3 The recorder\n- A ring buffer of the last 10 s at 60 Hz: each actor's rig input state (the object passed to `rig.update`), the ball state, the bails and particles' seeds, and the HUD values.\n- The **instant replay** after big moments (six, wicket, screamer, target smash, special) re-poses the rigs from the recorded states on the broadcast cam (or the cam that suits the moment), at 0.5× speed with a \"REPLAY\" bug and the ball's trail line. Replay frequency is a setting (Always / Big moments / Never); any button skips.\n- **Highlights reel:** the results screen keeps the recorded windows of the match's top 3 moments (scored by event weight), up to 3 × 6 s, in memory only.\n\n#### 5.11.4 Owl-Eye and the third umpire\n- **Owl-Eye** (LBW reviews, and the dev toggle \"Show trajectory\"): the ball path drawn as a translucent tube (`px.polyDither` segments) from release to impact, then the predicted continuation in a different colour to the stumps. Pitching and impact markers are discs on the pitch. Then a three-light verdict panel and the stamp.\n- **Third-umpire screen:** the review cam replays the crease at 0.25×, freezes at the moment the bails break, and zooms a \"TV\" frame overlay. A big OUT or NOT OUT reveal ticks between them like a slot reel for 1.2 s, then lands with crowd and commentary.\n\n#### 5.11.5 Tests\nThe director picks wide for a lofted drive and broadcast for a block; a review sequence completes and returns to `between`; the recorder replays a recorded six with the same ball positions within 0.5 units; the dev and test flag `warmViews` covers 4 bakes; bakes stay ≤ 10 entries during a full match (instrument `map.floors` size).\n\n### 5.12 Commentary and narrative text (`42-commentary.js`, `17-data-lines.js`)\n- **The commentators**:\n  - **Sir Aubrey Fennimore-Wolde**: plummy, unhurried, given to tangents about cake, pigeons, buses and his late Aunt Hermione;\n  - **Dazza Keegan**: a retired coastal fast bowler, blunt, \"mate\";\n  - in reviews only, **Professor Priya Statsworth**, the Owl-Eye analyst (\"Clipping the bails by a whisker of a whisker\").\n- **The director:**\n  - It subscribes to the bus, scores candidate lines by event priority, and plays at most one exchange per ball (1–2 lines, the second often the other commentator's reply).\n  - It respects `commentaryRate` *(T)* and stays quiet during the run-up and release; it talks from contact to `between`.\n  - Lines are picked from pools by tag, with a per-line cooldown (no repeat within 20 balls), from `RNG.cosmetic`.\n  - Template variables: `{batter} {bowler} {fielder} {team} {opp} {ground} {runs} {need} {balls} {target} {kmh} {speed} {item} {score}`.\n  - Idle filler (pigeons, cake from listeners, buses passing) plays at most once per over.\n- **Presentation:** an `E.Dialog` instance with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.6`, `place: 'bottom'`, alpha 0.85, and the speaker's portrait (a live Humanoid close-up), name in their colour. Text is typed with the gibberish voice (§5.13.3), at most 2 lines of ~42 characters, readable from 10 feet. Setting: Commentary captions on/off; voices have their own volume.\n- **Narrative voice everywhere:**\n  - Results show a 2–3-sentence **match report in Wisden style** (§8.9.3).\n  - Loading lines (\"Rolling the pitch\", \"Whitening the creases\", \"Brewing the tea\", \"Polishing the ball on a trouser leg\", \"Counting the crowd's umbrellas\").\n  - Menu tooltips, chapter cards and the Almanack-style ending all use the same dry, affectionate register.\n- **Tests:** every event type has ≥ 3 lines per relevant commentator; no line exceeds 2 × 42 characters after template fill with the longest names; no repeat within 20 balls in a 200-ball bot run; captions are off when the setting is off.\n\n### 5.13 Audio (`40-audio.js`, `41-voice.js`, `18-data-music.js`)\n\n#### 5.13.1 SFX\nEngine voices via `audio.define(name, voice)`, combining layers. Starting recipes:\n\n| Name | Recipe |\n|---|---|\n| `bat` (\"tock\") | `[{wave:'noise',freq:2400,to:900,dur:.04,vol:.35,filter:'bandpass'},{wave:'sine',freq:620,to:380,dur:.09,vol:.4},{wave:'triangle',freq:1240,dur:.05,vol:.15}]` |\n| `batSweet` | the above + `{wave:'sine',freq:880,to:1320,dur:.12,vol:.2}` (a ring for PERFECT) |\n| `edge` | `{wave:'noise',freq:5200,dur:.02,vol:.25,filter:'highpass'}` + a thin click |\n| `pitchThud` | `{wave:'sine',freq:140,to:80,dur:.06,vol:.3}` + soft noise |\n| `stumps` | wooden clatter: three short triangles at 700/540/860 Hz staggered 25 ms + noise; `bails`: two high ticks |\n| `gloves` | keeper's pop (low noise burst, lowpass) |\n| `throwWhoosh` | swept noise |\n| `dive` | a long lowpass noise slide |\n| `board` | ball hitting the boundary boards (wood knock) |\n| `glass` | smash: a noise burst plus random high sine tinkles from RNG.cosmetic |\n| `splash` | a noise burst falling from 3000 to 400 Hz (lowpass, 0.35 s) plus a low sine plop at 90 Hz, then a few soft droplet ticks |\n| `gull` | pitch-swept squawk with vibrato |\n| `moo` | a saw at 110→95 Hz, lowpass, vibrato |\n| `bees` | a buzzing saw with tremolo |\n| `steam` | the Thrumptonator: noise hiss plus a piston thump |\n| `firework` | rising whistle plus a boom |\n| `hypeFull`, `fireOn`, `special` | arpeggio stings |\n| menu | `select`, `confirm`, `cancel` (engine presets) |\n\nThrottle with the engine's 35 ms duplicate guard.\n\n#### 5.13.2 The crowd engine\nIts own Web Audio graph on `audio.bus('sfx')`:\n- an **ambient bed**: pink-ish noise through 3 bandpass filters (200, 800 and 2000 Hz), level = ground crowd size × 0.15, with a slow LFO murmur;\n- **applause**: dense random short noise grains (handclaps) at 30–90 per second, from RNG.cosmetic;\n- **cheer and roar**: a vowel-formant \"AAAH\" made from 6 detuned saws through formant filters (F1 750, F2 1250);\n- **\"OOOH\"** (F1 400, F2 800) for near misses, and a groan;\n- **chant**: \"KER-FUF-FLE\" as three formant syllables on the music beat (`audio.onBeat`), with claps.\n\nIntensity follows events (a six gives a roar of 2 s and 30 s of excited murmur). Rain mutes it to 30% plus umbrella rustle.\n\n#### 5.13.3 Gibberish voices (Animalese/Simlish style)\n`voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` turns text into syllables (vowel groups, with consonant onsets):\n- **Each syllable** is one source (saw for voiced sounds, plus a short noise burst for plosive and fricative onsets). It goes through two bandpass formant filters set to the syllable's vowel (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 300/2300, o 500/900, u 350/700 Hz; Q ≈ 6) and a gain envelope (attack 8 ms, hold, release 30 ms). Length is 55–110 ms × profile speed.\n- **Pitch** = profile base × (1 + small RNG.cosmetic jitter ±4%), × a declination of −12% across the phrase. It rises 25% at '?', with +30% and louder for '!' and CAPS. Gaps of 40 ms at spaces, 150 ms at punctuation.\n- **Profiles** (a blank speed means 1.0):\n\n  | Speaker | Base | Speed | Character |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | Aubrey | 105 Hz | 1.25 (slow) | vibrato 5 Hz |\n  | Dazza | 135 Hz | 0.85 | flat |\n  | Priya | 210 Hz | 0.9 | crisp |\n  | Edie | 240 Hz | 0.9 | |\n  | Basil | 120 Hz | | tremolo, wobbly |\n  | Dev | 170 Hz | | precise |\n  | Pip | 290 Hz | fast | |\n  | Mrs Crumb | 220 Hz | | warm, low Q |\n  | Rev. Hollis | 150 Hz | | soft |\n  | Thrumpton | 160 Hz | | nasal: formants ×1.2, glide |\n  | Ghosts | | | noise-voiced whisper, reverb (feedback delay 180 ms) |\n  | Thrumptonator | 90 Hz | | ring-modulated at 30 Hz |\n  | Players | | | random base 140–260 Hz |\n\n- **Uses:** the Dialog's typing calls `onSyllable` to sync the portrait's mouth. Short shouts (\"YES!\", \"NO!\", \"WAIT!\", \"HOWZAT!\", \"OUT!\") use the same synth with exaggerated pitch envelopes.\n- Route through `audio.bus('voice')` (engine change 3). Create the nodes lazily after the first gesture (the engine's `audio.ctx`).\n\n#### 5.13.4 Music\nOriginal songs in the engine's step notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes, name, on }] }`), each with named layers for dynamic intensity: `base`, `drums` (in when the Hype meter is > 50), `fire` (in when ON FIRE). Matches play the ground's track at low volume under the crowd. Between overs the music rises; during deliveries it ducks (`audio.layer`). Stings use `audio.sting(song, { at: 'beat' })`.\n\n| Track | Feel | Notes for the composer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `title` | \"The Kerfuffle March\": jaunty brass-band march, 120 bpm, square lead and triangle tuba bass, snare rolls | I–IV–V in B♭, a cheeky chromatic run every 4th bar |\n| `map` | the minibus travels: bouncy 6/8 | |\n| `green` | pastoral summer afternoon, 96 bpm, pulse-12 woodwind lead | cuckoo-like two-note motif |\n| `hall` | stately minuet with a harpsichord-like square arpeggio, 3/4 | |\n| `bay` | surf-rock: saw with vibrato as twangy guitar, driving drums, 150 bpm | |\n| `downs` | the conjurer's mystery waltz: minor key, tremolo | storm variant with rain drums |\n| `dome` | stadium rock anthem: chant-able hook, 128 bpm, layered | a spooky organ layer for the ghosts |\n| `tea` | gentle waltz for tea intervals and story scenes | |\n| `showdown` | a tense countdown loop | |\n| `attract` | a title remix | |\n\nStings: `four`, `six`, `wicket`, `over`, `hype`, `fire`, `special`, `win` (fanfare), `lose` (sad trombone slide), `toss`, `chapter`.\n\n#### 5.13.5 Tests\nHeadless audio is silent, so test the structure:\n- every `sfx()` name used in the code is defined;\n- `voice.syllables('Howzat!')` gives 2 syllables with a rising last pitch;\n- songs parse (each track's token count equals `steps × bars` for its length) and stay in their note range;\n- music layers switch on `fire:on`;\n- the `end` hub event mutes everything.\n\nIn a browser smoke run, `audio.failed` is null after a synthetic key press.\n\n### 5.14 Input and controls\n\n#### 5.14.1 The action map\nBuilt from `E.Input.DEFAULT` with the engine's attack/dash/skill/jump removed, these actions added, and menu confirm/cancel overridden so J selects and L backs out, matching gameplay.\n\n| Action | Batting | Bowling | Fielding | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move/aim | shot direction | pitch marker; aftertouch | steer the active fielder | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | pointer (aim and marker) |\n| `shot` | ground shot | delivery 1, meter release | catch, dive, throw to striker's end | ✕ (Pad0) | J | left click |\n| `loft` | lofted shot | delivery 2 | throw to bowler's end | □ (Pad2) | K | right click |\n| `block` | defend | delivery 3 | lob back to keeper | ○ (Pad1) | L, Q | — |\n| `special` | signature shot; REVIEW | signature delivery; REVIEW | — | △ (Pad3) | I, F | — |\n| `run` | run / again / dive | cycle field preset | switch fielder | R1 (Pad5) | Space | — |\n| `back` | stay / send back | over/round the wicket | — | L1 (Pad4) | Shift | — |\n| `charge` | charge down the pitch | — | — | R2 (Pad7) | E | — |\n| `pause` | engine | | | Options (Pad9) | Esc, P | corner button |\n| `confirm` / `cancel` (menus) | | | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space, J / Esc, Backspace, L | click / — |\n\n- Mouse clicks also pick menu items and things in the world: stumps to throw at, fielders in the field-preset preview, pins on the story map, and the review button.\n- **Hover moves the focus** in every menu (E.Menu does this; our widgets in `45-ui.js` must too).\n- The Backquote key (`) toggles the dev panel. It's keyboard-only and not rebindable.\n- Create (Pad8) is never bound (in hub sessions it belongs to the hub).\n- All rebindable through the engine's Controls screen. Pass `menu.controls` names: `{ shot: 'Ground shot / Bowl / Catch', loft: 'Lofted shot / Alt delivery', block: 'Defend / Alt delivery', special: 'Special / Review', run: 'Run / Field', back: 'Stay / Over-Round', charge: 'Charge' }`.\n- **Two players on one keyboard:** build each half's preset with `E.Input.halfKeys(KERFUFFLE_KEYS, 'left' | 'right')`. List the actions in the order shot, loft, block, run, back, special, so they land on F G H R T Y (left half) and on `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` (right half). halfKeys has six buttons, so add **charge** by hand: Q for the left half, Numpad0 for the right. Check neither collides, and that the mouse stays with player 1. The Controls screen shows a page per player.\n\n#### 5.14.2 Mouse feel\nIn batting, the aim arrow follows the pointer's ground point (`game.mouseGround()`). Left and right clicks are the two main shots, like the classic mouse-click browser cricket games PC players know. In bowling, the marker sits under the pointer, a click starts the run-up, and a click releases. In fielding, the active fielder runs toward the pointer while the right button is held (optional) and a click on stumps throws. The cursor is a small cricket-ball reticle drawn by the game; the system cursor is hidden over the canvas only in play.\n\n#### 5.14.3 Glyphs and prompts (`43-glyphs.js`)\n- `prompt(action)` draws an **icon** for the binding on `input.lastDevice`:\n  - **gamepad:** PlayStation glyphs by default. ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fd38a` shapes on dark discs, 9×9 px. Shoulder and trigger pills read L1 R1 L2 R2, and there are a stick and a d-pad icon. With the **Button symbols** setting on **Xbox**: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs, and LB RB LT RT pills. Read the symbol family with `input.padStyle()`, set by the engine's `glyphs` setting (engine change 4).\n  - **keyboard:** a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` ('J', 'Space', 'Shift').\n  - **mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit. Actions without a mouse binding fall back to their keycap.\n- Find the code from the action's bindings (`input.map[action]` after the player's rebinding) filtered by device: gamepad codes `Pad\\d+`, mouse `Mouse\\d`, else keys. Don't render `input.label()`'s strings ✕ ○ □ △ through `E.font`: the 5×7 font lacks those glyphs.\n- Prompts change on the very next frame after the device changes. The helper `prompts([['shot','GROUND'],['loft','LOFT']])` lays out a row.\n- **Tests:** after `press('KeyJ')` the shot prompt shows the keycap J; after `press('Pad0')` it's a PS cross; with the setting on xbox it's an A disc; after a mouse click the LMB icon; rebinding shot to KeyU shows U.\n\n### 5.15 UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n\n#### 5.15.1 Screen and type\n- `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (320×240 at least; a 16:9 TV shows 427×240), so every HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n- Body text uses the 5×7 font at scale 1 with a drop shadow (31 px tall on a 1080p TV). Headings use scale 2. Logos use `E.font.title`.\n- One decision per screen. At most 6 menu items. No tables of numbers, no scrolling lists of more than 8 rows, no paragraphs of more than 3 lines.\n- The **focus** is a big animated cricket-ball cursor plus a gold highlight bar. Every focusable element has a hover rectangle for the mouse.\n- Footer prompts on every screen: \"✕ Select ○ Back\", using live glyphs.\n- Keep the engine's top-right corner area clear (`game.cornerArea()`).\n\n#### 5.15.2 The match HUD (pixel overlay, `44-hud.js`)\n- **Score bug** (top left): the batting team's 3-letter code with its colour chip, a big `47/2`, and overs `3.4`. When chasing, a second line: \"NEED 12 FROM 8\" (gold if gettable, red if hard).\n- **This over** strip (top right): 6 balls as small discs (`•` dot, 1 2 3, 4 blue, 6 gold, W red, wd/nb grey).\n- **Striker card** (bottom left): a mini portrait plus \"PLUCKROSE 31* (14)\". **Bowler card** (bottom right): \"TWISTLETON 1-12\".\n- **Kerfuffle meter** (bottom centre): 120×6 bar, with \"△ SPECIAL\" when full and a flame overlay when ON FIRE.\n- **Transient callouts** (centre, big, 0.9 s): FOUR!, SIX! MAXIMUM!, WICKET!, FREE HIT, ON FIRE!, KERFUFFLE BONUS +10, PERFECT.\n- **Contextual prompts** sit near the action: \"R1 RUN / L1 STAY\" by the striker, the catch ring and \"✕ CATCH!\" at the ball, the throw picker icons at both stumps. Tutorial hints (chapter 1, Nets) show the full control row.\n- In bowling: the delivery panel (4 glyph buttons with names), the meter (140×10, bottom centre) and the speed readout after release.\n- In wide cam only: a **radar** (top right, under the over strip): an ellipse with fielders as team-colour dots and the ball as a white dot.\n- The commentary caption sits at the bottom centre, shown from contact onward, never during the run-up.\n- **Tests:** the HUD draws on 320×240, 427×240, 640×270 and 320×690 (via `--size` in the browser smoke) without overlapping the corner area or each other. Write a simple rectangle-overlap check in the HUD layout code, unit-tested.\n\n#### 5.15.3 Screens\n| Screen | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| Title | the village green at golden hour, the pavilion, a batter knocking up, Doris the cow, the logo, and \"PRESS\" with the confirm glyph for the last device. Then a menu of six: STORY (opens Continue, when a save exists, and New Game), QUICK MATCH, VERSUS, SUPER OVER SHOWDOWN, NETS, EXTRAS (opens Settings and About/Credits). |\n| Story map | a painted county map, 5 ground pins on a road `E.Path`, the minibus driving between them, 3 stars per chapter, Play / Replay |\n| Cutscene | a three-quarter vignette (the pavilion tea room, Thrumpton's car on the green) with posed rigs, `E.Dialog` with portraits and markup (`{shake}`, `{wave}`, `{c=#..}`), skippable with a hold-✕ ring (1 s) |\n| Match setup | big rows: Teams (left/right cycles a team card with kit and stars), Ground (a card with a panorama thumbnail), Overs 1–5, Wickets 2/3/5/10, Difficulty, Time of day, Weather, Kerfuffle rules, Players (join status). One row focused at a time, values change with left/right or a mouse click on the arrows. |\n| Toss | captains on the square, a coin flipped in 3D (sprite rotation frames), \"Call: ✕ HEADS ○ TAILS\", then \"BAT ✕ / BOWL ○\" |\n| Bowler select | 3–5 big portrait cards (type icon, overs left, special name) |\n| Innings break / Tea interval | \"TEA\" card, a summary in 3 big numbers (runs, wickets, top scorer), the target. In story, pick a tea treat (§8.7.1, 3 cards). |\n| Results | winner banner, margin, Player of the Match portrait, 3 key stats, the Wisden-style report (≤ 3 sentences), stars earned (story), \"▶ Highlights\" (the highlight reel), Rematch / Continue / Menu |\n| About / Credits | paged (◀ ▶): 1 Source Information, 2 Tech and Engine, 3 Art, Animation and Audio, 4 Online and Testing, 5 Testing Feedback, 6 Engine Feedback, 7 Prompt Feedback, 8 Credits. One topic per page, ≤ 10 short lines. |\n| Pause (engine) | Resume, our items (Restart Match, Match Info, Dev Mode), then the engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title. In hub sessions there is no Restart, Dev Mode or Quit (`menu.quit: false`); Full screen stays (the client hands it to the hub). |\n| Showdown | choose Bat or Bowl, play the over, then initials and a table (`E.Arcade.enter` / `drawTable`) |\n| Nets | lessons list, free practice (choose deliveries for the bowling machine or a dummy batter), Six Machine (12 balls, targets, score) |\n\n#### 5.15.4 Settings (`menu.settings`, group 'Game'; plus the engine's standard groups)\n\n| Setting | Values (default first) |\n|---|---|\n| Difficulty | County / Village / Test / Kerfuffle! |\n| Default overs | 2 / 1 / 3 / 5 |\n| Default wickets | 5 / 3 / 10 / 2 |\n| Kerfuffle rules | On / Off |\n| Pitch marker (batting) | On / Off |\n| Timing feedback | On / Off |\n| Fielding | Assisted / Manual / Auto |\n| Running | Manual / Auto |\n| Replays | Big moments / Always / Never |\n| Commentary captions | On / Off |\n| Commentary voices | On / Off |\n\nButton symbols live in the engine's Controller group (PlayStation by default, or Xbox). Voices volume lives in Sound (engine change 3). The Comfort group (reduce motion, shake, flashes) is the engine's, and it is honoured by all our FX.\n\n#### 5.15.5 Assists\nFielding: Assisted catches easy chances automatically, and Auto fields everything. Running: Auto follows the AI's YES and NO. Pitch marker. Difficulty. These go in Settings and in match setup.\n\n#### 5.15.6 Touch-only devices\n- **Detection:** `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, and no gamepad or keyboard event seen yet.\n- The game creates **no touch buttons** (no scene declares `touch`; set the engine's touch-button setting to hidden).\n- The title shows a large panel: **\"Controller or keyboard required\"**, a pad and keyboard pictogram, and a big **Settings** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`; the engine's corner menu button stays too). No other menu entries respond to taps.\n- **The attract mode still runs** after 10 s idle. A tap exits it back to this panel.\n- The moment a gamepad connects or a key is pressed, the panel goes and the normal title appears.\n- **Test:** headless with a stubbed `matchMedia` gives `snapshot().ui.touchGate === true`, no touch buttons, and attract starting after 10 s. A `press('Pad0')` clears the gate.\n\n### 5.16 Modes\n\n#### 5.16.1 Story: *The Grand Kerfuffle*\n5 chapters (§8.8) on the story map. Each chapter is a `MatchConfig` scenario plus scripted beats: `game.script` generators that wait on bus events, for example \"after innings 1, play the scene `thrumptonArrives`\". Each has 3 **Kerfuffle Goals** (stars), and a loss offers Retry or \"Retry on Village difficulty\".\n- Chapter 1 teaches through **lessons in the Nets** (§5.16.3), then plays the match with contextual tips.\n- Saves go through `game.saves`' autosave slot (§5.20), with `saves.write = () => ({ data: profile.story, place: chapterName, level: n })`. Don't set `menu: { saves: true }`: the story has no save points.\n- Quick Match unlocks a team when you beat it. Dev mode can unlock all.\n\n#### 5.16.2 Quick Match\nAny 2 of the 6 teams, any of the 5 grounds, plus the setup options. 1–4 players can join here (§5.16.5).\n\n#### 5.16.3 Nets\n- **Lessons** (scripted, each checks success before moving on):\n  1. Aim and ground shot.\n  2. Lofted shot.\n  3. Defend and leave.\n  4. Running (with a fielder).\n  5. Charge vs spin.\n  6. Bowling: marker and meter.\n  7. Aftertouch.\n  8. Specials and Hype.\n  9. Catch ring.\n  10. Throws.\n  11. Reviews.\n\n  Chapter 1 runs lessons 1–4 and 6. Later chapters run the others when first needed, from the chapter's beats.\n- **Free practice:** the bowling machine with chosen deliveries, line and length (or random), or bowling at a dummy batter.\n- **Six Machine:** 12 balls; score = runs + target bonuses; with a table.\n\n#### 5.16.4 Super Over Showdown\n- **Bat:** 6 balls from the Thrumptonator 3000 (seeded deliveries, a mixed set: express, bouncer, yorker, slower ball, banana swing, Triple Threat as the 6th ball), 2 wickets, Kerfuffle rules on. Score = runs.\n- **Bowl:** defend 14 against Kev Malone (a slogger), and score = (14 − runs conceded) × 10 + wickets × 25.\n- `E.Arcade` tables per variant with initials. The same Bat mode is the AI Hub round (§5.17).\n\n#### 5.16.5 Versus and local multiplayer (1–4 on one screen)\n- In match setup, players join through `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`: Start on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (which gives the keyboard's right half). Each joined player moves left or right to pick a team (player colours `E.PLAYER_COLORS`).\n- **Roles:**\n  - 1v1: each human does everything for their side.\n  - 2v2, or 2 humans co-op vs CPU: when batting, the two humans are the two batters. Each controls running for their own man, and the one on strike also plays the shots, so mix-ups are possible (§5.4). When bowling, they alternate overs, and the other takes the fielding (catch rings and throws).\n  - 2v1: the solo player does all roles for their side.\n- One shared camera, because cricket is a one-camera sport. Prompts appear by each player's actor in their colour, using their own device's glyphs (`players[i].lastDevice`).\n- The pause shows who paused (\"P2 PAUSED\", engine).\n- **Tests:** `game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })` gives P2 the bowler in a 1v1; co-op running agreement; mix-up run-out; the per-player prompt device.\n\n### 5.17 Online: AI Hub session (parallel Super Over) (`60-hub.js`)\n\n#### 5.17.1 Tags (in `<head>`, after the client)\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"parallel\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"2\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Most runs from one over (6 balls, 2 wickets) wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Hit: Cross/J/click · Loft: Square/K · Run: R1/Space\">\n```\nKeep the goal ≤ 60 characters and the howto ≤ 80 characters; the bundle test counts them.\n\n#### 5.17.2 Flow when `AIHub.active`\n1. Skip the title, attract and menus. Boot runs as normal. `AIHub.active` is already true on the first line, so decide at `new E.Game(...)`: pass `autoPause: false` (frames in the harness don't all have focus, and a paused copy would stall its round) and `menu: { quit: false, ... }`, and set `game.hubActive = true` (engine change 5).\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, start the normal standalone game.\n3. Build the round from `info.seed` via `AIHub.rng('over')`: the 6 deliveries (type, target, kmh), the ground (always the Megadome at night, so it looks the same for all), the batting side (Kerfuffle CC: Edie and Dev open, Pip comes in at the first wicket), and the field (the defensive preset).\n4. **Our card** (scene `hubcard`): the Megadome at night, the Thrumptonator steaming, and the players in their seat colours (`info.players`, names drawn as pixel text, never inserted as HTML) with READY ticks from the `card` event. Below: \"MOST RUNS FROM ONE OVER WINS\", this player's controls as glyph prompts for their last device, and the time left (`card.left`), plus \"Paused: most players want more time\" and \"Starting…\" states. ✕/Enter/click on READY calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △/T/click on MORE TIME calls `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card draws no `Math.random`: cosmetic noise comes from `AIHub.rng('card')`. It changes nothing in the round.\n5. Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the round is built, with no key press needed.\n6. On `start`: a countdown \"3, 2, 1, PLAY!\" while `AIHub.time() < 0`. The first ball's run-up begins at `time ≥ 0`.\n7. Play the over (scene `hubround`, the match machinery with `mode: 'hub'`). The pause menu has no Quit, Restart or Dev Mode (§5.15.3), and there are no replays (the clock is ticking).\n8. Call `AIHub.progress({ score: runs, label: runs + '/' + wkts + ' (' + balls + ' balls)' })` at the start and after every ball.\n9. The over ends after 6 legal balls or 2 wickets: call `AIHub.result({ score: runs, stats: { 'boundaries': b, 'sixes': s, 'best shot': name } })` once. Then show \"Waiting for the others…\" over the Megadome crowd.\n10. On `time-up`, if no result has been sent: call `result({ score: runs })` at once.\n11. On `end`: stop input (`game.input.clear()` plus a flag that ignores all actions), stop music and sfx (`audio.stopMusic()`, mute), and freeze the scene with \"Round over\", or \"You sat this one out\" for reason `sat-out`. The hub shows the results.\n12. Never call `send()`. No storage carries over (the client gives empty storage). No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`.\n\n#### 5.17.3 Tests\n- **hub-mock suite (Node):** a fake `AIHub` object before the game loads checks that `connect` is called with parallel and [1, 4], `ready` is called with no input, the deliveries are identical across two runs with the same seed and different player inputs, there's one progress per ball, one result, a time-up mid-over gives an immediate result, and `end` stops input and audio.\n- **browser-hub suite:**\n  1. Copy the bundle to `test/hub/game.html` beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js`.\n  2. Serve with `tools/serve.mjs` and confirm the title tag.\n  3. Run Playwright at `harness.html?game=game.html&players=1&auto=1`, then `players=2`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`.\n  4. Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` (90 s) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Print any failing check names and details.\n  5. Open the bundle outside a session (`file://`) and see the title scene.\n\n### 5.18 Attract mode (`51-attract.js`)\n- **Trigger:** on the title and every front-end menu scene (main menu, mode select, setup, story map), the `idle` timer resets on any input: an action press, any key, mouse movement (`input.mouse.t` changed), a click, any gamepad button or stick past the dead zone (`input.padMove`), or a touch. At **10 s** it calls `game.go('attract', { segment: next })`. Never in hub sessions.\n- **Exit:** any input (the same list) returns at once to the title and consumes the input (`input.consumeAll()`, so it doesn't also pick a menu item). The idle timer restarts. Nothing is saved; settings are untouched.\n- **What it shows:** a looping **showreel** of real gameplay on the real match machinery, with cheating autopilots on both sides (perfect timing, chosen outcomes, guaranteed catches, never-ending innings). It cycles through 10 segments (~25 s each), each a small data script `{ ground, timeOfDay, weather, teams, setup, balls: [{ delivery, target, shot, dir, grade, outcome, extra }] }`:\n  1. Village Green: Doris the cow ambles across the outfield; a cover drive runs through a Golden Teapot token (item collected) for FOUR (wide cam, applause, commentary).\n  2. A lofted straight SIX through the pub window (target smash, slow-mo, replay).\n  3. Basil's leg-break hits the pad: a huge appeal, given NOT OUT, the Kerfuffle Review System, Owl-Eye, OUT. The next batter is bowled by the googly (stumps cartwheel, the umpire's OUT signal).\n  4. Thrumpton Hall, overcast: an outswinger edged to slip, then a diving catch on a PERFECT ring.\n  5. Sunburnt Bay: a bouncer ducked, then the next one hooked for six into the sea, and the tide event.\n  6. A quick single, then a direct-hit run-out, third umpire, OUT.\n  7. Dustbowl Downs: the Vanishing Ball special, a stumping, then the rain delay with the covers and the Kerfuffle Method card.\n  8. Three boundaries, ON FIRE, the Tea-Kettle Slog special onto the jumbotron.\n  9. The Megadome at night in fog: the Thrumptonator's Triple Threat, ghost fielders, fireworks, a victory celebration and a Wisden-style report card.\n  10. The high-score table (`cab.drawTable`) and a \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" title flash.\n\n  Then it loops.\n- **On screen:** \"DEMO\" blinking (bottom centre) and \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the device-appropriate glyph), plus a TV-style caption per segment (the ground's name). Segments start with a wipe transition.\n- **Tests:**\n  - **attract-coverage suite:** idle 10 s on the title gives the attract scene. Advance 12 minutes of game time: every segment ran at least once and the coverage flags were set (item, chaos, four, six, target, review, bowled, caught, run-out, third umpire, stumping, special, ON FIRE, rain, fog, ghosts, night), with no errors.\n  - Then `press('KeyA')` returns to the title, a mouse move (`input.feed('move', ...)`) exits, and `press('Pad3')` exits.\n  - Touch-only: attract runs and a tap exits.\n\n### 5.19 Dev mode (`70-dev.js`)\n- **Reach:**\n  - the **pause menu item \"Dev Mode\"** (`menu.items`) in every pausable scene;\n  - Backquote as a keyboard shortcut;\n  - hidden in hub sessions.\n\n  It opens as a full-screen 10-foot panel over the paused game. Values apply instantly. Resume to see them in play.\n- **Navigation:** L1 and R1 (or Tab, or a click on a tab) switch tabs. Up and down (or hover) select a row. Left and right adjust; hold to accelerate; the mouse drags slider knobs and the wheel scrolls. ✕ toggles or activates, △ resets the selected row to its default, ○ goes back.\n  - **\"Reset all to defaults\"** is the first item of the TUNING tab.\n  - A **\"DEV\" badge** shows on the HUD while any value differs from its default.\n- **Persistence:** tuning persists through `E.store.set('kerfuffle:dev', ...)` (dev mode is hidden in hub sessions, whose storage is memory-only anyway).\n\n#### 5.19.1 Tabs\n1. **TUNING** (sliders, each showing its current value against the default):\n\n   | Key | Label | Default | Min | Max | Step |","numLines":420,"startLine":420,"totalLines":1435}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018vBn14FEaLfW4qMYVGhtg2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
