{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6b2BUePvWguLeP1B7a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Cricket Kerfuffle** is finished: `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-062757.html` and `exit-interview.json` (which parses, with every field answered) are in this folder's root. The final full run of `node test/run.mjs` gives **288 passed, 0 failed (17 suites)**. No process I started is still running.\n\nTo answer the stop-hook concerns directly:\n\n- **A new suite, `task`, checks TASK.md line by line on the built game:**\n  - the file name matches and there are no audio files in it;\n  - a whole flow on a gamepad alone (title → menu → setup → toss → match → a clean shot with Cross);\n  - PlayStation glyphs by default;\n  - Options pauses, and Dev Mode opens from the pause menu with live sliders and a reset;\n  - five chapters plus the ending;\n  - gibberish voices;\n  - the four About sections;\n  - hub tags and local joining.\n- **Every one of the 21 specials** now has its effect checked in a test, including Dibbly-Dobbly Doom (an 80 km/h wobbling ball that doubles the CPU's timing error) and Sawn in Half (a googly whose bowled wicket splits the stumps). Neither is cut down.\n- **The story ending exists and is reached:** the campaign suite wins chapters 1–5 through the autopilot, plays the final Megadome scene and lands on the credits.\n- **Gamepad and touch** are exercised with simulated pad buttons and taps, including the \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen and attract mode running on it. I still haven't held a real controller or touch device.\n- **No TODOs, FIXMEs, placeholders or stubs** in the source; a test greps for them.\n- **The plan deviations** I listed (the rain-delay formula, chapter 3's chase of 26, the default run speed) are complete working choices, not missing pieces. TASK.md takes precedence over the plan, and every TASK.md requirement is in.\n\nOne real bug turned up in this last pass: in AI Hub rounds, delivery speeds could differ slightly between players' copies. Each delivery is now drawn from the round's seed, so every copy faces identical balls. The browser suite also crashed twice from low memory on the shared machine (about 2 GB free). It crashed the same way without the hub fix, so it wasn't the game. It now loads a blank page between test pages and passes, including the AI Hub harness with 1, 2 and 4 players.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:27:57\n- **Source:** Cricket (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story campaign. 2.5D: a TV camera behind the bowler for every delivery, a high wide camera following the ball, close-ups and a side-on review camera; a three-quarter view for story scenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio; tests on Node and Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's pixel-art character style at 320×240 (expanded to fill the screen), canvas lighting, engine shapes and materials, and new cricket assets (stumps, sight screens, the pub, the Thrumptonator, Doris the cow, a painted backdrop of the far stands).\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, reposed with IK for two-handed bat swings by shot type, the overarm bowling action and umpire signals; engine throws, poses and dives.\n- **Audio:** Music in layers (drums with the Hype meter, a fire layer when ON FIRE), synthesized effects, a crowd engine and Animalese-style gibberish voices, all in code.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1: a parallel Super Over at the Megadome for 1–4 players, with the game's own card.\n- **Testing:** Headless engine (`game.advance`, `game.debug`), scripted playthroughs and CPU bot matches behind one command, Playwright browser checks, the AI Hub harness.\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine's handoff docs and kits, and the AI Hub README and example.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 42: roughly 26 gameplay or logic bugs, 7 visual problems, 6 balance problems and a few mistakes in my own tests.\n2. **How many were critical?** 8:\n   - a dead ball that never ended;\n   - two scenes (cutscenes and the match) that could only finish once;\n   - a misnamed dead-ball handler;\n   - the camera aimed thousands of units away;\n   - an engine crash drawing the ground at the low TV camera angle;\n   - long throws counted as overthrows;\n   - a commentary crash in CPU-only matches.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The forced-outcome scenarios (field, arcade, attract). They run the real ball-by-ball machinery end to end.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that bowled balls land where aimed. It mostly measured the simulation's step size.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** The character rig, whose pose step can be wrapped with IK.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** A custom view stores its pitch in radians (`pitchDeg` holds degrees). A long press made the click check come back empty in a headless test.\n3. **Built myself:** The cricket simulation, fielding, running, umpiring and reviews, and the forced-outcome layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** The foreshortened TV camera: the ground drawing broke at a low angle, and the far end needed a painted backdrop.\n5. **Changes:**\n   - a cricket bat with a two-handed grip;\n   - batting pads, moustaches, beards and glasses;\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default, with Xbox as the option;\n   - Create + Options left to the AI Hub;\n   - ten cached ground drawings (was six);\n   - a fix for the ground-drawing cache crash.\n6. **One change:** A perspective (telephoto) camera option for ground views.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** The testable definition of done, and the freedom to change the engine.\n2. **Confusing:** How binding a very large plan is when TASK.md wins on disagreements.\n3. **Hardest:** Fitting the full game and its testing into one session.\n4. **Would change:** Give a target scope, and say which plan features are required and which are optional."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":729416,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":500},"output_tokens":732,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"0e617773-7447-4c47-b9ef-3b0b95b906fe","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:07:51.732Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi6b1kvNCQvsqsAGq4y5"}
