{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6x6Jquugvi2N1rh1wL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've added real-browser input tests, and the game now passes **296 of 296 checks (17 suites)** with `node test/run.mjs`. The game file and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root, the interview parses with every field answered, and no server I started is still running.\n\n**Input is now tested in Chromium through the browser's own input paths.** Nothing is injected into the game itself:\n- **Touch-only device** (an emulated phone with touch and no mouse):\n  - \"Controller or keyboard required\" is shown.\n  - A tap on its Settings button opens the real Settings screen. I checked that this screen is absent before the tap.\n  - The attract mode starts after the idle time, and a tap exits it.\n- **Gamepad**, polled through `navigator.getGamepads()`:\n  - Cross opens the menu, and the prompts switch to PlayStation glyphs.\n  - D-pad down and Cross reach Quick Match setup.\n- **Mouse and keyboard:**\n  - Hovering moves the menu focus, and a click picks Nets.\n  - Key presses play deliveries without errors.\n\nThis is still emulation. I have no physical controller or touch screen here, which is why TASK.md's Testing section points to headless tools and scripted playthroughs.\n\n**On the \"partial features\" concerns:**\n- **Story ending:** chapter 5's ending plays and closes on the credits. The campaign suite reaches it by playing all five chapters.\n- **Specials:** each of the 21 has its effect checked in a test, including Sawn in Half and Dibbly-Dobbly Doom. None is cut down.\n- **No stubs:** besides the grep for TODO/FIXME/placeholder, the TASK.md suite checks requirements in the built game and a gamepad-only playthrough.\n- **Plan deviations:** these are not missing features. The rain-delay formula scales by total resources, chapter 3's chase is 26 instead of 30, and the batters' default speed is 135 instead of 120. Each is a balance choice, and every one of these systems is in the game.\n\nBoth remaining judgements are mine: that the deviations are fair balance choices, and that emulated input stands in for real devices. Nothing independent checks either.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:27:57\n- **Source:** Cricket (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story campaign. A TV camera behind the bowler for every delivery, a wide camera following the ball, close-ups and a side-on review camera, plus a three-quarter view for story scenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio; tests on Node and Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's pixel-art characters at 320×240 (expanded to fill the screen), canvas lighting, engine shapes and materials, and new cricket assets.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, with IK posing for two-handed bat swings, the overarm bowling action and umpire signals.\n- **Audio:** Music in layers, synthesized effects, a crowd engine and Animalese-style gibberish voices, all made in code.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1: a parallel Super Over for 1–4 players, with the game's own card.\n- **Testing:** Headless engine (`game.advance`, `game.debug`), scripted playthroughs and CPU bot matches behind one command, Playwright browser checks including emulated touch, gamepad and mouse input, and the AI Hub harness.\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine's handoff docs and kits, and the AI Hub README and example.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 42: roughly 26 gameplay or logic bugs, 7 visual, 6 balance, and a few errors in my own tests.\n2. **How many were critical?** 8:\n   - a dead ball that never ended;\n   - two scenes that could only finish once;\n   - a misnamed dead-ball handler;\n   - the camera aimed far off the ground;\n   - an engine crash drawing the ground at the low camera angle;\n   - long throws counted as overthrows;\n   - a commentary crash in CPU-only matches.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The forced-outcome scenarios, which run the real ball-by-ball machinery end to end.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that deliveries land where aimed, which mostly measured the simulation's step size.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** The character rig; its pose step can be wrapped to add IK.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** A custom view keeps its pitch in radians (`pitchDeg` holds degrees). A long press left the click check empty in a headless test.\n3. **Built myself:** The cricket simulation, fielding and running, umpiring and reviews, and the forced-outcome layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** The low TV camera: the ground drawing broke at that angle, and the far end needed a painted backdrop.\n5. **Changed:**\n   - a cricket bat with a two-handed grip;\n   - batting pads, moustaches, beards and glasses;\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default, with Xbox as the option;\n   - Create + Options left to the AI Hub;\n   - ten cached ground drawings instead of six;\n   - a fix for the ground-drawing cache crash.\n6. **One change:** A perspective (telephoto) camera option for ground views.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** The testable definition of done, and the freedom to change the engine.\n2. **Confusing:** How binding a very large plan is when TASK.md wins on disagreements.\n3. **Hardest:** Fitting the full game and its testing into one session.\n4. **Would change:** Give a target scope, and say which plan features are required."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":715,"cache_read_input_tokens":736686,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":715},"output_tokens":707,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"21c274f2-30d2-4a0f-a6e9-dc586c5f9017","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:12:34.835Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi6x5n7L9VEAu1nmgjZa"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The transcript does not show that every TASK.md feature is fully implemented. The game file and exit-interview.json exist, parse, and the final reply has all four sections. However, the assistant's own final message admits 'Both remaining judgements are mine' and that the plan deviations are its own 'balance choice' assessment, with 'Nothing independent checks either'. The no-stubs claim rests on a grep for TODO/FIXME/placeholder plus self-written tests. Testing was emulated input (no real gamepad or touch device), and earlier feedback flagged partially modelled specials and the story ending, which the assistant only re-asserted rather than independently verified. The stop condition's requirement that every feature is implemented is therefore not demonstrated by the transcript evidence."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"545d9604-3eac-4c35-9b61-7066df831e99","uuid":"f306a122-6b7b-43a4-8eb5-7e1328835ce1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:12:36.836Z","isSynthetic":true}
